\id MRK - Open English Translation—Readers’ Version (OET-RV) v0.5.14
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Mark
\toc1 The account of Jesus’s ministry by Mark
\toc2 Mark
\mt2 The account of Jesus’s ministry by
\mt1 Mark
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\c 1
\m
\v 1 \add Here\add* begins the good message about\f + \fr 1:1 \ft As well as ‘about’, the Greek construction can also have the connotation of ‘brought by’, or even imply both. (\xt John 1:1 \ft also confirms that Jesus was both the messenger and the message itself.)\f* God’s son, Jesus \add the\add* messiah:
\s1 The ministry of John the immerser (The return of ‘Elijah’)
\r (Mat. 3:1-12; Luke 3:1-18; Yhn. 1:19-28)
\p
\v 2 The prophet Isaiah wrote:
\q1 “Look, I’m sending my messenger ahead of you,
\q1 \add the one\add* who’ll be preparing your path.
\q1
\v 3 The voice \add out\add* in the wilderness shouting,
\q2 ‘Get the path ready for Yahweh—
\q2 make it smooth for him \add to walk on\add*.’ ”
\p
\v 4 John turned up in the wilderness announcing that people should get immersed \add in water\add* in order to show that their sins have been forgiven,
\v 5 and many from the Judea region and from Jerusalem \add city\add* went out to \add hear\add* him, and \add asked John\add* to immerse them in the Jordan river as they confessed their sins.
\p
\v 6 John was dressed in \add simple clothes made from\add* camel hair with a leather belt around his waist, and he lived on locusts and wild honey.
\v 7 He also announced, “There’s a greater \add man\add* coming soon—\add in fact\add* I’m not even good enough to bend down and untie his sandal straps.
\v 8 I immersed you all in water, but he’ll immerse you in God’s holy spirit.”
\s1 Jesus gets immersed and then tempted
\r (Mat. 3:13–4:11; Luke 3:21-22; 4:1-13)
\p
\v 9 \fig Jesus was immersed by John.|src="images/41_Mk_01_06_RG.jpg" size="col" loc="1:9" copy="© Sweet Publishing" ref="1:9"\fig*Then one day Jesus came from Nazareth in the Galilee \add region\add*, and he got immersed in the Jordan \add river\add* by John.
\v 10 As Jesus came up out of the water, he saw the sky being opened up, and God’s spirit coming down on him like a dove.
\v 11 And a voice came from the sky \add saying\add*, “You’re my son who I love \add and\add* am pleased with.”
\p
\v 12 After that, the spirit sent him out into the wilderness
\v 13 where he was tested by Satan for forty days. He was out there with the wild animals, but God’s messengers\f + \fr 1:13 \ft The Greek doesn’t actually distinguish between human messengers and heavenly messengers (so-called ‘angels’—the Greek word just transliterated letter-by-letter instead of being actually translated).\f* looked after him \add there\add*.
\s1 The selection of the first followers/apprentices
\r (Mat. 4:12-22; Luke 4:14-15; 5:1-11)
\p
\v 14 After John had been arrested, Jesus went back up \add north\add* to Galilee, announcing the good news from God
\v 15 saying, “\wj The promised time is here, and God’s kingdom is \+add now\+add* near. Turn from your sins and believe this good news.\wj*”
\p
\v 16 As he went around Galilee lake, Jesus saw \add two\add* fishermen, Simon and his brother Andrew, throwing a net into the lake
\v 17 and he said to them, “\wj Come with me and I’ll make you into fishers of people.\wj*”
\v 18 So they left their fishing work and went off with Jesus.
\p
\v 19 Then a bit further on he saw \add two\add* of Zebedee’s sons, James and his brother John, in a boat getting the nets ready.
\v 20 Jesus called them and they \add too\add* went off with him, leaving their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired workers.
\s1 Jesus drives out a demon
\r (Luke 4:31-37)
\p
\v 21 They went on into Capernaum where as soon as a rest day started, he would go into the Jewish meeting hall \add (Grk. synagogue)\add* \add and\add* teach \add there\add*.
\v 22 The congregation was amazed at his teaching, because he taught like someone with authority, not like the regular teachers \add there\add*.
\p
\v 23 Then a demon-possessed man in the meeting hall suddenly yelled out,
\v 24 “What are we to you, Jesus of Nazareth? You’ve come to destroy us. I know that you’re God’s holy one.”
\p
\v 25 But Jesus scolded it,\f + \fr 1:25 \ft The Greek pronoun is masculine, implying that the man was the one being scolded, yet “Come out of him!” implies that the spirit is being spoken to.\f* saying, “\wj Keep quiet!\wj*”, and, “\wj Come out of him!\wj*”
\p
\v 26 Then the demon threw the man into a convulsion, cried out loudly, and left him.
\v 27 Everyone was astounded and asked each other, “What just happened? We haven’t heard anything like this before—he has authority to command demons, and they obey him!”
\v 28 So then the news about Jesus spread all over that entire Galilee region.
\s1 Jesus heals many others
\r (Mat. 8:14-17; Luke 4:38-41)
\p
\v 29 After leaving the meeting hall, Jesus went with James and John into the home of Simon and Andrew.
\v 30 Inside, Simon’s mother-in-law was lying down with a fever, and the others spoke to Jesus about her.
\v 31 When he went up to her, and took hold of her hand \add and\add* helped her up, the fever left her and she prepared something for them to eat.
\p
\v 32 That evening when the sun set, the people brought all those who were sick or demon-possessed to Jesus.
\v 33 \add It seemed like\add* the entire town had gathered there outside the \add front\add* door.
\v 34 Jesus healed many who were sick from a range of diseases, and he commanded many demons to leave—not allowing them to speak because they knew that he was \add the\add* messiah.
\s1 Jesus prays before going preaching
\r (Luke 4:42-44)
\p
\v 35 In the small hours of the morning, Jesus got up and went out to a quiet place and prayed there.
\v 36 But Simon and the others \add came\add* looking for him,
\v 37 and \add when\add* they found him they said, “Everyone’s looking for you!”
\p
\v 38 “\wj \+add Well,\+add*\wj*” Jesus answered, “\wj we’ll be going into the other villages around here, so that I can also tell them. That’s what I came here for.\wj*”
\v 39 Then he went preaching in all their meeting halls in Galilee, and driving out demons.
\s1 Jesus heals a man with leprosy
\r (Mat. 8:1-4; Luke 5:12-16)
\p
\v 40 One day, a man with leprosy came to Jesus asking him, “I know that you could heal me if you wanted to.”
\p
\v 41 Jesus felt compassion \add for him\add*, reached out his hand and touched him, and said, “\wj Yes, I will. Get well.\wj*”
\v 42 Then immediately the leprosy disappeared and he was well again.
\v 43 So Jesus sent him away, \add but\add* warned him:
\v 44 “\wj Don’t talk to anyone—just go to the priest and show him \+add that you’re well\+add*, and as a testimony of that, make the offering that Moses commanded so as to be declared clean again.\wj*”
\p
\v 45 But after the man left, he started telling lots of people and spreading the news—\add so much\add* so that Jesus could no longer come into the town but had to stay out in quieter places, and people kept coming to him.
\c 2
\s1 Jesus heals a paralysed man
\r (Mat. 9:1-8; Luke 5:17-26)
\p
\v 1 When Jesus went back to Capernaum after a few days, \add people soon\add* heard that he was \add back\add* in the house
\v 2 and \add so\add* many people turned up there that there wasn’t any room left—not even in the doorway. \add Inside,\add* Jesus was explaining the scriptures to them.
\v 3 Four men arrived, carrying a paralysed man to Jesus,
\v 4 but because of the crowd, they were unable to get close. \add So\add* they \add climbed up onto the flat\add* roof, removed a section, and lowered the paralysed man lying on a pallet.
\v 5 Jesus, observing their faith, said to the man, “\wj Son, your sins are forgiven!\wj*”\f + \fr 2:5 \ft Jesus’s hearers would have taken this as a claim to be God, as they knew that only God could forgive sins as v7 shows.\f*
\p
\v 6 But some of the religious teachers were sitting there, thinking to themselves,
\v 7 “Why’s that fellow talking like that? He’s seriously insulting God. There’s only one who can forgive sins, and that’s God \add himself\add*.”
\p
\v 8 Then Jesus, knowing in his spirit that they were reasoning like that, said to them, “\wj Why are you thinking that to yourselves?\wj*
\v 9 \wj Which is easier: to tell the paralysed man that his sins have been forgiven, or to tell him to pick up his bedding and walk?\wj*
\v 10 \wj But so you all can see that the son of humanity \+em does\+em* have authority here on the earth to forgive sins,\wj*” he \add turned and\add* said to the paralysed man,
\v 11 “\wj Listen. Get up, take your bedding, and go home!\wj*”
\p
\v 12 So the man stood up, and picked up his bedding, and went outside, where everyone saw him and marvelled and praised God saying, “We’ve never seen anything like that \add happen before\add*!”
\s1 Levi’s (Matthew’s) calling
\r (Mat. 9:9-13; Luke 5:27-32)
\p
\v 13 Jesus went back out beside the lake where crowds of people kept coming to him and he taught them.
\v 14 As he was passing by the tax office, he saw Levi Alphaeus sitting \add there\add* and said to him, “Come along with me.” So Levi stood up and followed Jesus.
\p
\v 15 Jesus ended up having a meal at Levi’s place, and many \add other\add* tax collectors and undesirables were sitting \add there also\add*, along with Jesus and his followers, because \add by then\add*, many \add people\add* were following him around.
\v 16 When the teachers from the Pharisee party noticed that Jesus was eating beside undesirables and tax-collectors, they asked his followers, “\add How come\add* he eats with tax-collectors and other low-life?”
\p
\v 17 Jesus overheard them and said, “\wj Healthy people don’t need a doctor—it’s the sick ones that do. I didn’t come \+add here\+add* to help those who think they have no needs, but I came to call sinners.\wj*”
\s1 The question of fasting
\r (Mat. 9:14-17; Luke 5:33-39)
\p
\v 18 Now the followers of John-the-immerser, and the Pharisee party members, \add regularly\add* fasted. So a small group came to Jesus and asked, “How come John’s followers and the Pharisee party fast \add regularly\add*, yet your followers don’t?”
\p
\v 19 “\wj Can the groomsmen fast when they’re \+add celebrating\+add* along with him?\wj*” Jesus answered, “\wj \+add No,\+add* as long as he’s still there, they won’t be fasting.\wj*
\v 20 \wj But in the future, the groom will be taken away from them, and \+em then\+em* they’ll be fasting.\wj*
\p
\v 21 “\wj No one sews a patch of \+add new\+add*, unshrunken cloth onto used clothes, because the \+add new\+add* patch will tear away from that old fabric, and it will end up with a worse tear.\wj*
\v 22 \wj And no one puts new wine into old wineskin \+add containers\+add*, because the wine will burst the \+add already stretched\+add* wineskins, and then both the wine and the wineskins will be destroyed. New wine must be put into \+em new\+em* wineskins.\wj*”
\s1 Working on the Rest Day
\r (Mat. 12:1-8; Luke 6:1-5)
\p
\v 23 Then it so happened that Jesus and his followers passed through some fields of grain on some days of rest. As they went through, they plucked off some heads of grain,
\v 24 and \add some of\add* the Pharisee party members said, “Hey, it’s breaking the law to harvest grain on a rest day!”
\p
\v 25 “\wj You’ve read \+add yourselves\+add*,\wj*” Jesus replied, “\wj what David did when he and his men were hungry.\wj*
\v 26 \wj They went into God’s tent and ate the \+add sacred\+add* bread \+add representing\+add* God’s presence. Only the priests were allowed to eat that, and yet David even gave some to his companions!\wj*”
\v 27 Then he told the Pharisee party, “\wj The days of rest were created for people, not people for the rest days.\wj*
\v 28 \wj So \+add I\+add*, the son of humanity, have authority also over the day of rest.\wj*”
\c 3
\s1 The man with the curled-up hand
\r (Mat. 12:9-14; Luke 6:6-11)
\p
\v 1 Again \add at another time\add*, Jesus went into the Jewish meeting hall and there was a man there with a curled-up hand.
\v 2 The religious leaders there were watching him closely \add to see\add* if he would heal the man on the rest day, and then if he did, they would have a case against him.
\v 3 Then Jesus said to the man with the flawed hand, “\wj Stand up there in the middle.\wj*”
\v 4 And he asked the leaders, “\wj Is it allowable to do good on the rest day, or \+add only\+add* to do evil? To save life, or to kill?\wj*” But they didn’t reply.
\p
\v 5 After angrily looking around at them, grieving at their complete lack of compassion, he told the man, “\wj Open up your hand!\wj*” The man stretched out his fingers, and his hand became totally normal.
\v 6 But the Pharisee party quickly left, and meeting with \add King\add* Herod’s supporters, they conspired to kill Jesus.
\s1 Big crowds press against Jesus
\p
\v 7 After that, Jesus withdrew towards the lake with his apprentices as well as a huge crowd \add of people\add* from the \add regions of both\add* Galilee and \add down in\add* Judea,
\v 8 along with others from Jerusalem, Idumea, from across the Jordan \add river\add*, and from \add up\add* near Tyre and Sidon. That huge crowd came \add because\add* they had heard about the \add amazing\add* things he had done.
\v 9 So Jesus asked his apprentices \add to organise\add* a small boat to be there for him so he wouldn’t be cramped by \add the press of\add* the crowd.
\v 10 Virtually anyone who had any physical issues was pressing towards him to touch him, because he had \add already\add* healed so many.
\v 11 And the demons, whenever they saw Jesus, they fell to their knees on the ground in front of him and yelled out, “You are God’s son!”
\v 12 However he \add regularly\add* warned them not to reveal who he was.
\s1 Jesus appoints his twelve representatives
\r (Mat. 10:1-4; Luke 6:12-16)
\p
\v 13 As Jesus \add started\add* going up a hill, he called the followers that he had selected and they went off with him.
\v 14 He appointed twelve \add of them\add* as his representatives so that they could stick with him, and be sent off to be declaring \add his message\add*
\v 15 and to also have authority to exorcise demons.
\p
\v 16 \add These are\add* the twelve that he appointed: Simon but he appended the name “Peter”),
\v 17 James and his brother John who were Zebedee’s sons (and he nicknamed them the “sons of thunder”),
\v 18 Andrew, Philip, Bartholomew, Matthew, Thomas, James Alphaeus, Thaddeus, Simon from Canaan,
\v 19 and Judas Iscariot who \add later\add* betrayed him.
\s1 The two opposing spiritual powers
\r (Mat. 12:22-32; Luke 11:14-23; 12:10)
\p
\v 20 Then Jesus was approaching a house, but the crowd swarmed them again so they had no opportunity to eat.
\v 21 The ones with him heard all the noise and came to get him, because they were wondering if he was crazy.
\p
\v 22 The religious teachers had come up from Jerusalem and were complaining, “He’s possessed by the prince of demons. He can command them because he rules them!”
\p
\v 23 But Jesus addressed them: “\wj How could Satan command himself?\wj*
\v 24 \wj If a kingdom is divided against itself, that kingdom could never prosper.\wj*
\v 25 \wj If a household is divided against itself, that family will never prosper.\wj*
\v 26 \wj So if Satan attacked himself and was split, he couldn’t possibly be powerful, but his time would be over.\wj*
\p
\v 27 \wj On the contrary, no one can enter a strong man’s house to clean out his possessions unless he first ties up that strong man—after that he can work on the goods.\wj*
\p
\v 28 \wj Honestly, I assure you that any sins of a person can be forgiven, no matter how much they curse \+add God\+add*.\wj*
\v 29 \wj But anyone who speaks against the Holy Spirit can never be forgiven, but is eternally responsible for their sin.\wj*”
\v 30 (\add He said that\add* because they were saying that he was demon-possessed.)
\s1 Jesus’s mother and brothers arrive
\r (Mat. 12:46-50; Luke 8:19-21)
\p
\v 31 Now Jesus’ mother and brothers came and were standing outside, and they asked someone to tell him that they were there.
\v 32 \add Some in\add* the crowd sitting around him told Jesus that his mother and brothers were waiting outside for him.
\p
\v 33 But Jesus answered, “\wj Who is my mother and who are my brothers?\wj*”
\v 34 And having looked around at the people sitting here, he said, “\wj Actually, you all are my mother and my brothers,\wj*
\v 35 \wj because anyone who obeys God, that person is my brother and my sister and my mother.\wj*”
\c 4
\s1 The parable about planting seed
\r (Mat. 13:1-9; Luke 8:4-8)
\p
\v 1 Again Jesus started teaching by the edge of the lake. A huge crowd gathered around him, but he had boarded a boat that was floating in the water, and all the people were \add there\add* on the shore.
\v 2 From there, he taught them many \add different\add* things in parables, including this one:
\v 3 “\wj Listen. Once there was a farmer who went to spread some seed.\wj*
\v 4 \wj As he spread it, some landed on the path, and the birds came and fed on it.\wj*
\v 5 \wj Some landed on a rocky part without a lot of soil, and it quickly sprouted in the \+add warm\+add*, shallow soil,\wj*
\v 6 \wj but when the sun came up, it dried out because the roots weren’t deep, \+add and so\+add* it \+add just\+add* withered up.\wj*
\v 7 \wj Some of the seed landed in a thistle patch, and the thistles grew and choked it out, so the plants never bore a crop.\wj*
\v 8 \wj But some \+add seed\+add* landed in fertile soil and it bore a harvest—\+add some of\+add* it multiplying thirty \+add times\+add*, some sixty, and some a hundred \+add times\+add*.\wj*”
\p
\v 9 Then Jesus added, “\wj Anyone who wants to understand, let them consider it.\wj*”
\s1 Why Jesus speaks in parables
\r (Mat. 13:10-17; Luke 8:9-10)
\p
\v 10 Later when the crowd had left, the twelve apprentices asked him about the parables
\v 11 and he told them, “\wj You’ve been given understanding about God’s kingdom, but for others, they’ll get it in parables\wj*
\q1
\v 12 \wj so that when they look, they might see but not perceive,\wj*
\q1 \wj and when they listen, they might hear but not understand,\wj*
\q1 \wj otherwise they might turn \+add from their sins\+add* and be forgiven.\wj*”
\s1 The ground where the seed fell
\r (Mat. 13:18-23; Luke 8:11-15)
\p
\v 13 Then Jesus said to them, “\wj If you didn’t understand this parable, how will you understand any others?\wj*
\v 14 \wj The person spreading seed is spreading the \+add good\+add* message,\wj*
\v 15 \wj and there are some along the path where the \+add good\+add* message is being spread, who hear, \+add but\+add* Satan turns up immediately and snatches the message that had been planted in them.\wj*
\v 16 \wj Similarly, there’s some seed that falls onto rocky places where the people who hear the \+add good\+add* message happily accept it,\wj*
\v 17 \wj but don’t make strong roots—just temporary \+add changes\+add*. So when they get mocked or attacked about accepting the \+add good\+add* message, they quickly stumble.\wj*
\v 18 \wj Others are the ones where the seed was spread among the thistles. They heard the \+add good message\+add*,\wj*
\v 19 \wj but contemporary topics, or the lure of money, or the appetite for other things, choke their good intentions and the message becomes unfruitful \+add in them\+add*.\wj*
\v 20 \wj But \+add lastly\+add*, the seed that landed on the good soil represents \+add the people\+add* who hear the \+add good\+add* message, accept it, and are fruitful thirty, sixty, or \+add even\+add* a hundred times \+add over\+add*.\wj*”
\s1 The light will shine on everything
\r (Luke 8:16-18)
\p
\v 21 Then Jesus told them, “\wj Obviously no one goes and gets a lamp, and then puts it under a basket or under the bed, but rather they place it \+add somewhere higher\+add* on a stand.\wj*
\v 22 \wj You see, nothing is hidden that won’t \+add eventually\+add* be disclosed, and there’s no secret that won’t \+add eventually\+add* be revealed.\wj*
\v 23 \wj Anyone who wants to understand must listen carefully,\wj*”
\v 24 and he continued, “\wj Take care what you listen to. How much you all judge others, so too will judgement be handed out to you, and even more.\wj* DOUBLE-CHECK
\v 25 \wj Anyone who has \+add faith\+add* will be given \+add a reward\+add*, but if you have none, even the little you have will be taken away.\wj*” DOUBLE-CHECK
\s1 The parable about the sprouting seed
\p
\v 26 And then, “\wj God’s kingdom is like a person throwing \+add some\+add* seed onto the ground.\wj*
\v 27 \wj Whether the person sleeps \+add at night\+add* or stays up night and day, the seed will sprout and grow \+add either way\+add*, and they don’t know how \+add it does it\+add*.\wj*
\v 28 \wj The earth is \+add designed\+add* to multiply the seed: first a sprout \+add appears\+add*, then a bigger plant, and then the full head of grain.\wj*
\v 29 \wj Then when it’s ripe, the harvester is sent in because it’s ready for harvest.\wj*”
\s1 The small seed produces a large result
\r (Mat. 13:31-32,34; Luke 13:18-19)
\p
\v 30 Again he said, “\wj What could we liken God’s kingdom to? Or what parable could we explain it with?\wj*
\v 31 \wj It’s like planting a mustard seed in the ground—a very small seed.\wj*
\v 32 \wj But when it’s planted and grows up, it forms a large bush with many branches so that birds can nest in its shade.\wj*”
\p
\v 33 And so as much as they could handle it, Jesus shared the \add good\add* message with them via many similar word pictures.
\v 34 He never spoke without those illustrations, but he explained all of them to his \add close\add* followers.
\s1 Jesus halts the storm on the lake
\r (Mat. 8:23-27; Luke 8:22-25)
\p
\v 35 It was already becoming evening on that day, \add so\add* Jesus told his apprentices, “\wj Let’s \+add all\+add* go across to the other shore.\wj*”
\v 36 Then after sending the crowd away, they got into the boat with him, plus there were \add some\add* other boats with them.
\v 37 \add Out on the lake,\add* a big storm came up with strong winds, and the waves started breaking across the boat and filling it \add with water\add*.
\v 38 However, Jesus was asleep on a cushion in the back, so they woke him, calling out, “Teacher, don’t you care that we’re all about to drown?”
\p
\v 39 Now awake, Jesus scolded the wind and waves, “\wj Quieten down! Stop that!\wj*” Then the wind died down and \add the lake\add* became very calm.
\v 40 \add Turning back to them\add* he asked, “\wj Why were you all afraid? Don’t you have \+add any\+add* faith yet?\wj*”
\p
\v 41 But now they were very afraid \add again\add*, and asked each other, “Who could this man be that both the wind and the waves obey him?”
\c 5
\s1 Jesus sends the demons into pigs
\r (Mat. 8:28-34; Luke 8:26-39)
\p
\v 1 So they got \add safely\add* to the Gerasenes region on the other side of the lake.
\v 2 As Jesus climbed out of the boat, a demon-possessed man \add came\add* out of the tombs to meet him.
\v 3 He lived in those tombs, and no one could tie him up, not even with chains,
\v 4 because he had often been tied up \add in the past\add* with shackles and chains but had always pulled the chains apart and smashed the shackles, and no one was able to subdue him.
\v 5 All through the day and night, \add whether\add* in the tombs or \add up\add* in the hills, he was yelling out and hurting himself with stones.
\p
\v 6 So when he had seen Jesus in the distance, he ran and \add as a sign of honour\add* fell to \add his knees on the ground\add* in front of him.
\v 7 Then he called out very loudly, “What will happen between us, Jesus—the son of the highest God? I beg you in God’s \add name\add* not to torment me.”
\v 8 Because Jesus had \add already started\add* saying, “\wj Come out of the man you ungodly spirit!\wj*”
\p
\v 9 Jesus asked, “\wj What’s your name?\wj*”
\p The man answered, “My name is ‘Thousands’ because there’s many of us,”
\v 10 and he begged Jesus not to send them out of the country.
\p
\v 11 It just so happened that there was a large herd of pigs being fed near the hill,
\v 12 and the demons begged Jesus \add again\add* saying, “Send us to the pigs so that we can enter them.”
\v 13 Jesus agreed to that, \add so\add* the ungodly spirits came out \add of the man\add*, went into the pigs, and the \add entire\add* herd of some two thousand \add pigs\add* rushed down the steep bank into the lake and were drowned.
\p
\v 14 The ones who had been feeding them rushed off and spread the news around in the city and the surrounding area, So \add the people\add* hurried out to see what had happened.
\v 15 When they arrived where Jesus was, they saw the man who had been demon-possessed, \add fully-\add*clothed and sitting there in full control of his senses. \add They knew it was\add* the man called ‘Thousands’ and they became frightened.
\v 16 Those who had seen it all happen described it \add all\add* to the people—what had happened \add both\add* to the demon-possessed man and to the pigs.
\p
\v 17 So then the people \add began\add* asking Jesus to go away from their region.
\p
\v 18 As Jesus was climbing back into the boat, the man who had been demon-possessed wanted to go with him.
\v 19 But Jesus wouldn’t let him and told him to head back home to his own people, and explain to them what the master had done for him, and \add how\add* he had been merciful to him.
\p
\v 20 So he went away and shared with \add the people in\add* the Ten Cities \add region\add* what Jesus had done for him, and they were all amazed.
\s1 A woman healed as Jesus goes to help a dying girl
\r (Mat. 9:18-26; Luke 8:40-56)
\p
\v 21 When Jesus had crossed in the boat to the other side \add of the lake\add*, a large crowd \add of people\add* came out to the lake edge to see him.
\v 22 Then unexpectedly, one of the religious leaders named Jairus came out, and when he found Jesus, he fell to his knees in front of him
\v 23 and pleaded, “My young daughter is \add critical and\add* just hanging on for you to come and touch her so that she’ll be healed and live.”
\p
\v 24 So Jesus went off with Jairus. The large crowd followed \add also\add*, and many were getting pressed against Jesus.
\p
\v 25 Then a woman \add came who\add* had been losing blood for twelve years,
\v 26 and who’d endured much under various doctors despite spending everything she had. After all that, she’d ended up even worse,
\v 27 \add but\add* when she heard about what Jesus \add had been doing\add*, she worked her way through the crowd to the back of \add Jesus and\add* touched his clothes,
\v 28 because she had told \add herself\add*, “If I could even just touch his clothes, I’d be healed.”
\p
\v 29 Immediately the bleeding stopped, and she knew that she had been healed.
\v 30 Immediately also, Jesus knew that power had gone out of him, \add and\add* he turned back towards the crowd \add and\add* said, “\wj Who touched my clothes?\wj*”
\p
\v 31 But his followers said, “You can see all the people that were pressing on you so why would you ask who touched you?”
\p
\v 32 But Jesus was \add still\add* looking around to see who had done it
\v 33 when the woman, scared and shaking because of what was happening, came and fell \add to her knees\add* in front of Jesus and told him the whole story.
\v 34 “\wj Daughter, your faith has healed you,\wj*” Jesus said. “\wj Go in peace and full health.\wj*”
\p
\v 35 While he was still speaking, messengers came from the religious leader’s house to tell Jairus, “Your daughter has died. Why bother the teacher any more?”
\p
\v 36 But Jesus overheard them telling that to the leader and told him, “\wj Don’t be afraid—just keep believing.\wj*”
\v 37 Jesus didn’t allow anyone to accompany him other than Peter, and James and his brother, John.
\v 38 As they approached Jairus’ house, Jesus saw a \add big\add* commotion \add ahead of them\add*, with lots of wailing and crying \add going on\add*.
\v 39 As he entered, he said, “\wj Why are you all crying in distress? The child hasn’t died—she’s \+add just\+add* sleeping!\wj*”
\p
\v 40 But they \add just\add* ridiculed Jesus, so he sent them all outside, \add just\add* taking the father and mother of the child, along with three with him. He went in where the girl was,
\v 41 and taking her hand, he said, “\wj Young woman, I tell you: get up!\wj*”
\v 42 Immediately she got up and walked around, and everyone marvelled at the amazing thing that had happened. (She was twelve years old.)
\v 43 Jesus repeatedly told them to keep this to themselves, and added that \add they\add* should give her \add something\add* to eat.
\c 6
\s1 Jesus’s community can’t believe he’s from God
\r (Mat. 13:53-58; Luke 4:16-30)
\p
\v 1 So Jesus left Capernaum and went back with his followers to Nazareth, his birthplace.
\v 2 It was now the Rest Day and when he started teaching in the Jewish meeting hall, many who heard him were astonished, saying, “Where did that man learn all that? How could he have such wisdom and be able to do those miracles?
\v 3 Isn’t that the carpenter, the son of Mary, and the brother of James and Joseph CHECK and Judas and Simon? Don’t his sisters live around here?” And they were offended by his \add teaching\add*.
\p
\v 4 Then Jesus told them, “\wj A prophet is only dishonoured in his \+add own\+add* home town, and among his \+add own\+add* relatives, and in his \+add own\+add* home.\wj*”
\p
\v 5 He wasn’t able to do \add even\add* one miracle there, except for placing his hands on a few sick people and healing them.
\v 6 He was amazed at how they wouldn’t believe him as he went around the villages teaching.
\s1 Jesus sends his apprentices out in pairs
\r (Mat. 10:5-15; Luke 9:1-6)
\p
\v 7 Then he called his twelve apprentices and started sending them out in pairs and giving them authority over demons.
\v 8 He insisted that they can carry a walking stick, but not to take food, a backpack, or money.
\v 9 They could wear one pair of sandals, but not take changes of clothes,
\v 10 and he told them, “\wj Whenever you’re invited into a home, stay at that one place until you all leave that town.\wj*
\v 11 \wj But if any places don’t want you or don’t want to hear what you have to say, \+add just\+add* leave that place. \+add As you go,\+add* shake the dust off your sandals so they can see \+add that you pass the responsibility of your leaving, onto them\+add*.\wj*”
\p
\v 12 So the apprentices went off and \add started\add* telling people to repent.
\v 13 In addition, they drove out many demons, and anointed many sick people with olive oil and healed them.
\s1 The execution of John-the-immerser
\r (Mat. 14:1-12; Luke 9:7-9)
\p
\v 14 Jesus was now becoming well known and King Herod \add Antipas\add* heard about him. He thought that maybe John-the-immerser had come back to life and that’s why Jesus was able to do miracles.
\p
\v 15 But others thought it was \add the promised return of\add* Elijah, and \add yet\add* others thought he must be \add another\add* prophet like the ones \add in the scriptures\add*.
\p
\v 16 But hearing \add about Jesus\add*, Herod was telling people, “That John who I \add had\add* beheaded, he must have come back to life!”
\v 17 Because Herod himself had had John arrested and put in prison because he had married Herodias, his brother Philip’s wife.
\v 18 John had been telling the king that it was wrong for him to marry his brother’s wife,
\v 19 and \add so\add* Herodias resented him \add enough to\add* want to kill him, but Herod hadn’t be able to
\v 20 because he respected John as a righteous and holy teacher and \add so\add* he kept him alive. He \add often\add* enjoyed hearing from John and thought a lot \add about what he said\add*.
\p
\v 21 However, an opportunity came at Herod’s birthday celebration, when he threw a feast for his top officials and generals, and for the leaders in Galilee.
\v 22 The daughter of Herodias came in and danced \add for them\add*, and the king and his party guests all enjoyed it. So the king said to the young woman, “\add Just\add* ask me for anything and I’ll give it to you.”
\v 23 Then he promised her, “Even if you asked for half of my kingdom, I’d give it to you!”
\p
\v 24 So she went out and asked her mother what she should ask for. Her mother told \add her that she should ask for\add* John’s head.
\p
\v 25 So going back into \add the party\add*, she said seriously to the king, “I would like the head of John-the-immerser right now on a plate!”
\p
\v 26 The king was really upset \add with that\add*, \add but\add* because he’d made loud promises in front of all his guests, he couldn’t say no.
\v 27 So he ordered an executioner to go out and return with John’s head. He went off and beheaded him in the prison
\v 28 and brought the head \add into the party\add* on a plate and gave it to the girl, and she gave it to her mother.
\v 29 When John’s followers heard about that, they came and fetched his body and put it into a tomb.
\s1 Jesus feeds 5,000 people
\r (Yhn 6:1-14; Mat. 14:13-21; Luke 9:10-17)
\p
\v 30 Now the apprentices \add who had been sent out,\add* returned back to Jesus, and they reported \add back\add* everything they had done and what they had been teaching \add people\add*.
\v 31 Then he told them to head to a quiet place with him to take a break. \add That was\add* because there were so many people always coming and going, and \add sometimes\add* they didn’t even have a chance to eat.
\v 32 So they went away by boat to a quiet place by themselves.
\p
\v 33 But people from towns all around had seen them going and recognised who they were, so they ran around the lake on foot and got there ahead of them.
\v 34 So when Jesus got out \add of the boat\add*, he saw a big crowd and felt sorry for them because they seemed like sheep without a shepherd, so he started teaching them many things.
\v 35 Then, when it was already getting late, his apprentices came up and said, “This place is pretty isolated and it’s already late.
\v 36 Send these people off \add now\add* so they can go to the nearby settlements and villages and buy themselves something to eat.”
\p
\v 37 But Jesus answered them, “\wj You give them something to eat.\wj*”
\p They complained, “If we go, should we buy several months wages worth of bread so we can give \add all of\add* them something to eat?”
\p
\v 38 But he asked, “\wj Go and find out how much bread you all have.\wj*”
\p When they’d found out they said, “Five buns and two fish.”
\p
\v 39 Jesus told all the people to line up in rows \add and\add* sit down on the green grass.
\v 40 So they sat down in rows of fifty to a hundred.
\v 41 Then holding the five buns and two fish, he looked up to the sky and blessed \add God\add*, and then broke up the buns and gave \add pieces\add* to his apprentices so they could do likewise to the people, and he \add also\add* divided up the two fish,
\v 42 and all the people ate and were satisfied.
\v 43 Then they collected the leftover pieces \add of bread\add*, filling twelve baskets, as well as the leftover pieces of fish.
\v 44 The \add headcount of\add* men who ate \add that evening\add* was five thousand.
\s1 Jesus walks on the lake surface
\r (Yhn 6:15-21; Mat. 14:22-33)
\p
\v 45 Straight after that, Jesus sent his apprentices off in the boat to go across to Bethsaida while he sent the crowd \add home\add*.
\v 46 Then he said goodbye to everyone, \add and\add* climbed the hill to pray.
\v 47 Later in the night, the boat was out in the middle of the lake, and only Jesus remained back there on the land.
\v 48 He saw them struggling to row against the wind, and some time after 3am he came \add out\add* to them, walking on the lake \add surface\add* as if to walk past them.
\v 49 But they had seen someone walking on the lake and thought it was a ghost, and they yelled out
\v 50 because they had all seen it and were terrified.
\p But Jesus immediately spoke to them and told them, “\wj \+add Hey,\+add* take heart, it’s me, don’t be afraid!\wj*”
\v 51 Then he climbed up into the boat and the wind died down. They were all totally astounded
\v 52 because they hadn’t understood the implications of him feeding all the people because their minds were closed.
\s1 Jesus heals many people around Gennesaret
\r (Mat. 14:34-36)
\p
\v 53 After completing their crossing, they anchored at Gennesaret.
\v 54 When they got out of the boat, again \add people\add* recognised Jesus
\v 55 and ran around that region, so next minute people began \add bringing all of\add* those who were sick, carrying them on pallets to wherever they heard that Jesus was.
\v 56 After that, whenever Jesus entered villages or cities, or fields or marketplaces, they laid the sick \add down there\add* and were imploring him that they might even just touch the edge of his robe, and anyone who did so was healed.
\c 7
\s1 Jesus suggests obeying God, not tradition
\r (Mat. 15:1-9)
\p
\v 1 \add Some from\add* the Pharisee party and some of the law teachers had come from Jerusalem to observe Jesus
\v 2 and noticed that some of his followers were eating their food \add without following their religious customs, i.e.,\add* without washing their hands.
\v 3 (The Pharisee party and, \add in fact,\add* all the Jewish \add religious leaders\add* don’t eat without \add first ceremonially\add* washing their hands as taught by their elders \add over the generations\add*.
\v 4 They won’t eat at the market if they can’t do that \add first\add*, and have many other regulations about \add ceremonial\add* washing of cups and jugs and copper containers.)
\p
\v 5 Then the Pharisee party and religious teachers asked Jesus, “How come your followers don’t respect our Jewish traditions, but eat without the ceremonial washing of hands?”
\p
\v 6 “\wj Isaiah got it exactly right,\wj*” he answered, “\wj when he expressed \+add God’s thoughts\+add* about you hypocrites and wrote:\wj*
\q1 \wj ‘These people honour me with their lips,\wj*
\q2 ^\wj but their real desires are far away from me.\wj*
\q1
\v 7 \wj Their worship of me is worthless—\wj*
\q2 \wj teaching man-made rules as if they are from God.’\wj*
\p
\v 8 \wj You’ve ignored God’s commands instead accepting human traditions.\wj*
\p
\v 9 “\wj You all \+add simply\+add* dismiss God’s commands in favour of your own customs,\wj* Jesus continued.
\v 10 \wj Moses teaches \+add us\+add*,\wj*
\q1 \wj ‘Honour your parents,’\wj*
\q1 \wj and\wj*
\q1 \wj ‘Anyone dishonestly slandering their parents deserves the death penalty.’\wj*
\m
\v 11 \wj But \+add when you all encourage people\+add* to say, ‘Whatever I had that may have helped you guys has been given \+add to God\+add*’,\wj*
\v 12 \wj you are actively discouraging them from helping their parents—\wj*
\v 13 \wj overriding God’s message with your \+add very convenient\+add* teaching that you disseminate instead. And that’s not the only example!\wj*”
\s1 Evil comes from inside a person
\r (Mat. 15:10-20)
\p
\v 14 Jesus called out to the crowd, “\wj Everyone listen to me carefully:\wj*
\v 15 \wj Nothing that enters your mouth makes you guilty in God’s eyes. It’s what comes \+em out\+em* of you that demonstrates your culpability.\wj*”
\p
\v 16 ◘
\v 17 When Jesus came back inside, his apprentices asked him what he meant.
\v 18 “\wj Can’t you understand something so simple,\wj*” he scolded them. “\wj Can’t you see that anything from the outside that enters your body doesn’t make you guilty in God’s sight,\wj*
\v 19 \wj because it doesn’t enter your mind but \+add merely\+add* goes into your stomach and ends up in the toilet.\wj*” (\add By saying this he clearly showed that\add* it was ok \add for Jews\add* \add to eat\add* any food.)
\p
\v 20 “\wj It’s what comes out of you that makes you guilty in God’s eyes,\wj*” he continued.
\v 21-22 “\wj That’s because evil thoughts, sexual immorality, theft, murder, adultery, greed, wickedness, deceit, arrogance, lust, slander, pride, and foolishness all come from \+em inside\+em* people’s hearts \+add and minds\+add*.\wj*
\v 23 \wj \+add So\+add* it’s the evil that’s inside \+add a person\+add* and comes out that makes them guilty \+add in God’s eyes\+add*.\wj*”
\s1 The bold faith of a non-Jewish woman
\r (Mat. 15:21-28)
\p
\v 24 Jesus left that place and went \add up north\add* into the regions of Tyre and Sidon. He tried to enter a house without being noticed but didn’t succeed,
\v 25 and straight away a woman who had heard about him turned up. She knelt in front of him because she had a young daughter who was demon-possessed.
\v 26 The woman was Greek from Phoenicia in Syria, and she begged Jesus to command the demon to leave her daughter.
\v 27 “\wj Let the \+add Jewish\+add* children be treated first,\wj*” he told her, “\wj because it’s not good to take the children’s food and give it to the household dogs.\wj*”
\p
\v 28 “Yes, master,” she responded, “but the puppies under the table \em do\em* get to eat the little crumbs that the children \add drop\add*.”
\p
\v 29 “\wj \+add Ha,\+add* go home \+add now\+add*\wj*” he answered, “\wj As a result of how you just spoke, the demon has \+add already\+add* left your daughter.\wj*”
\p
\v 30 And when she got home, the demon had indeed gone and the young girl was lying \add quietly\add* on her bed.
\s1 Jesus heals a deaf man who couldn’t speak
\p
\v 31 Jesus left the Tyre region, passed through Sidon, and came down the \add eastern\add* side of Galilee lake to the Decapolis region.
\v 32 The people there brought a man to him who couldn’t hear or speak, and asked Jesus to heal him.
\v 33 So Jesus took the man back away from the crowd, put his fingers in the man’s ears, then he spat and touched the man’s tongue.
\v 34 Looking up to the sky, he sighed and said, “\wj Open up!\wj*”
\p
\v 35 Then the man could hear, and his tongue was released and he \add began to\add* speak properly.
\v 36 Then Jesus advised that they shouldn’t tell anyone, but the more he said that, the more they seemed to spread it around.
\v 37 The crowds were totally amazed, saying, “He can do anything—he makes the deaf able to hear, and enables those who couldn’t speak.”
\c 8
\s1 Jesus feeds 4,000 people
\r (Mat. 15:32-39)
\p
\v 1 Another time there was a large crowd again with nothing to eat. Jesus called his apprentices and told them,
\v 2 “\wj I feel sorry for all these people because they’ve already been with us for three days and haven’t had anything to eat.\wj*
\v 3 \wj I can’t send them home because they’re too weak to walk, and some have a long way to go.\wj*”
\p
\v 4 “Where could anyone find enough food \add out\add* here in the wilderness?” his apprentices queried.
\p
\v 5 “\wj How much bread have you all got?\wj*” he asked.
\p “Seven \add buns\add*,” they answered.
\p
\v 6 So Jesus instructed the crowd to sit down on the ground. Then he took the seven buns, gave thanks and pulled them apart, giving pieces to his apprentices to then distribute to the crowd.
\v 7 They also had a few small \add cooked\add* fish, so he blessed them \add too\add* and got them to distribute them as well.
\v 8 After all the people had eaten and were full, they collected seven basketfuls of leftovers.
\v 9 There were some four thousand people there \add that day\add*, and Jesus sent them home \add after\add* they’d eaten.
\v 10 Then he and his apprentices climbed into the boat and headed for the Dalmanutha region.
\s1 The religious leaders want to see a miracle
\r (Mat. 16:1-4)
\p
\v 11 \add Some men from\add* the Pharisee party came out \add to Jesus\add* and started arguing with him. They were checking him out and wanted to see him do a miracle.
\v 12 Jesus grieved inside and asked, “\wj Why do you people need to see a miracle \+add before you’ll believe me\+add*? Actually, no miracle will be done for this generation.\wj*”
\p
\v 13 So Jesus left in the boat again and crossed to the other side of the lake.
\s1 The apprentices miss the significance of what’s happening
\r (Mat. 16:5-12)
\p
\v 14 But Jesus’ apprentices forgot to take any food other than one bun that someone in the boat had.
\v 15 He told them, “\wj Watch out for the yeast of the Pharisee party and of \+add King\+add* Herod.\wj*”
\p
\v 16 They asked each other what it meant—was it because they forgot food?
\p
\v 17 But Jesus knew what they were saying and asked, “\wj Why are you all discussing about not having food? Can’t you figure it out? Or are your minds completely closed?\wj*
\v 18 \wj You all have eyes but can’t seem to see and have ears but can’t seem to listen. Have you already forgotten what just happened?\wj*
\v 19 \wj When I broke up the five buns to feed five thousand people, how many basketfuls of leftovers did you collect?\wj*”
\p “Twelve,” they answered.
\p
\v 20 “\wj And when we fed four thousand with seven buns, how many full baskets of leftovers then?\wj*”
\p “Seven,” they answered.
\p
\v 21 “\wj So how can you all fail to understand \+add what’s going on right in front of you\+add*,\wj*” Jesus asked.
\s1 Jesus heals a blind man near Bethsaida
\p
\v 22 As they entered Bethsaida, the people brought a blind man to Jesus and asked him to touch him.
\v 23 So Jesus took the man’s hand and led him out of the village. Then he spat in his eyes, placed his hands on him, and asked him, “\wj Can you see anything?\wj*”
\p
\v 24 The man looked around and answered, “I can see the people, but they just look like trees.”
\v 25 Jesus touched the man’s eyes again and then he could see clearly—he was now completely better.
\v 26 Then Jesus told him not to go back into the village but to go straight home.
\s1 Jesus asks who they think he is
\r (Mat. 16:13-20; Luke 9:18-21)
\p
\v 27 Then Jesus and his apprentices moved around the \add various\add* villages of Caesarea-Filippi. On the way, he asked them, “\wj Who does everyone think I am?\wj*”
\p
\v 28 “\add Some say\add* John-the-immerser,” they said, “some \add say\add* Elijah, and some \add say\add* one of the prophets.”
\p
\v 29 “\wj And what do you all think?\wj*” he asked.
\p “You are the promised one \add sent by God\add*,” Peter answered him.
\p
\v 30 Then Jesus warned them not to tell others about him.
\s1 Jesus’s radical challenge to his would-be followers
\r (Mat. 16:21-28; Luke 9:22-27)
\p
\v 31 Then Jesus began teaching his apprentices that the son of humanity would need to suffer a lot, to be rejected by the religious leaders, and so to be put to death but to come back to life after three days.
\v 32 He taught this \add very\add* clearly, but Peter took him aside and told him off.
\v 33 Jesus turned back and looked at the others, then reprimanded Peter, “\wj Stand back, Satan—you’re applying human values instead of considering God’s plan.\wj*”
\p
\v 34 Calling out to them and to the crowd, he said, “\wj Anyone who wants to follow me must first put aside their own desires and then follow me—even through very difficult times.\wj*
\v 35 \wj Anyone who worries about saving their own life will lose it, but anyone who gives their life due to following me and my teaching will save their life.\wj*
\v 36 \wj No, it doesn’t benefit a person if they gain \+add everything in\+add* the world yet lose their soul.\wj*
\v 37 \wj What can someone offer in order to buy a home for their soul?\wj*
\v 38 \wj Yes, anyone who’s ashamed of me and my message \+add in front of\+add* this adulterous and sinful generation, the son of humanity will likewise be ashamed of them when he comes clearly displaying his father’s greatness and bringing the holy messengers.\wj*”
\c 9
\p
\v 1 Then he continued, “\wj I can assure you that some of the ones standing here won’t die until they’ve seen God’s powerful kingdom arrive.\wj*”
\s1 Moses and Elijah speak with Jesus
\r (Mat. 17:1-13; Luke 9:28-36)
\p
\v 2 Six days later, Jesus took Peter and James and John, and just the four of them went up onto a high hill. Then he was transformed right in front of their eyes
\v 3 and his clothes turned incredibly shining white—whiter than any bleach could make them.
\v 4 Then Elijah and Moses appeared beside Jesus and \add started\add* talking with him.
\v 5 Peter said to Jesus, “Teacher, it’s good that we’re here. Maybe we could make three shelters \add up here\add*: one for you, one for Moses, and one for Elijah?”
\v 6 \add He said that\add* because they were terrified \add and\add* didn’t know what to say.
\p
\v 7 Then a cloud shadowed them \add from the sun\add*, and a voice came out of the cloud \add saying\add*, “This is my dear son. Listen to him.”
\v 8 When they looked around, they couldn’t see anyone \add else there\add* anymore, except for Jesus himself.
\p
\v 9 As they came down the hill \add again\add*, Jesus told them not to tell anyone what had happened up there until after the son of humanity had come back to life.
\p
\v 10 They remembered that, although between all three of them they couldn’t understand the bit about coming back to life,
\v 11 so they asked Jesus, “Isn’t it written that Elijah will be the first to return?”
\p
\v 12 “\wj \+add Yes,\+add*\wj*” he answered, “\wj Elijah is indeed the one to come first to \+add start\+add* the restoration. But isn’t it also written that the son of humanity will be despised and will suffer a lot?\wj*
\v 13 \wj You see, Elijah has come \+add already\+add*, and they treated him just however they liked, \+add just\+add* like it was written that it would happen.\wj*”
\s1 Jesus drives out a stubborn demon
\r (Mat. 17:14-21; Luke 9:37-43a)
\p
\v 14 When they got \add back\add* to his \add other\add* followers, Jesus saw that they were surrounded by a large crowd and the religious teachers were debating with them.
\v 15 The crowd were very surprised when they noticed Jesus and \add came\add* running to greet him.
\v 16 He asked them, “\wj What were you all arguing with them about?\wj*”
\p
\v 17 One of the people answered, “Teacher, I brought my son here—he has a demon that prevents him from speaking.
\v 18 Whenever it takes hold of him, it attacks him and he starts foaming \add at the mouth\add* and grating his teeth and his joints lock up. I tried to get your followers to command it to leave but they couldn’t.”
\p
\v 19 “\wj You’re a faithless generation,\wj*” Jesus replied. “\wj How much longer will I have to be here? How much longer can I tolerate you all? Bring him here.\wj*”
\v 20 So they brought the boy to him. When he saw Jesus, the demon immediately caused him to have convulsions and fall on the ground—rolling \add over\add* and foaming \add at the mouth\add*.
\p
\v 21 Jesus asked the boy’s father, “\wj How long has this been happening for?\wj*”
\p “Since he was small,” he replied,
\v 22 “and often it throws him into the fire or into water to try to destroy him. But if can do anything and you feel sorry for us, \add please\add* help us.”
\p
\v 23 Jesus asked him, “\wj If I can? A person who believes can do anything.\wj*”
\p
\v 24 The boy’s father cried out, “I do believe—help me \add to fight\add* unbelief.”
\p
\v 25 Then Jesus noticing that a \add big\add* crowd was quickly forming, scolded the evil spirit, “\wj You spirit who causes muteness and deafness, leave this boy \+add now\+add* and never return to him.\wj*”
\p
\v 26 There was yelling and strong convulsions, and then the demon left him and the boy lay still as if dead, and many people thought he was.
\v 27 However Jesus took his hand and lifted it, and the boy got up.
\p
\v 28 When Jesus and his apprentices had gone into the house by themselves, they asked him, “\add How come\add* we couldn’t drive it out?”
\p
\v 29 “\wj That kind will only leave after prayer and fasting,\wj*” he replied.
\s1 Jesus talks again about being executed
\r (Mat. 17:22-23; Luke 9:43b-45)
\p
\v 30 Then they left that place and were moving around Galilee, because Jesus didn’t want people to know where he was
\v 31 while he was teaching his apprentices. He told them, “\wj The son of humanity will be arrested and executed, but after three days he’ll come back to life.\wj*”
\p
\v 32 But they didn’t know what that meant, and they were too scared to ask him.
\s1 How to be the best
\r (Mat. 18:1-5; Luke 9:46-48)
\p
\v 33 Then they went to Capernaum and when they arrived at the house, Jesus asked them what they were discussing on the way.
\p
\v 34 But they didn’t reply because while they were walking, they’d been talking about which of them is the best.
\v 35 So Jesus sat down \add and\add* called the twelve \add of them\add* and explained, “\wj Anyone who wants to be at the top, has to be at the bottom and help all the others.\wj*”
\v 36 Then Jesus called a young boy \add and\add* stood him in the middle of them, and giving him a hug said,
\v 37 “\wj Anyone who welcomes young children on my behalf, welcomes me, and anyone who welcomes me is not \+add actually\+add* welcoming me, but welcoming the one who sent me here.\wj*”
\s1 Others are also serving Jesus
\r (Luke 9:49-50)
\p
\v 38 John checked with Jesus, “Teacher, we saw someone using your authority to command demons to leave, but we told him off because he wasn’t one of us.”
\p
\v 39 Jesus responded, “\wj No, don’t stop him, because anyone who does a miracle using my name won’t just quickly \+add turn around and\+add* slander me.\wj*
\v 40 \wj Yes, anyone who’s not against us is for us.\wj*
\v 41 \wj Anyone who gives you water to drink because you’re followers of God’s chosen one and who does it because they’re working for me, I can assure you that they’ll receive their reward.\wj*
\s1 Difficult teachings about disobedience
\r (Mat. 18:6-9; Luke 17:1-2)
\p
\v 42 “\wj But anyone who causes a young person to falter from their trust in me, it would be better if they were thrown into the sea with a heavy weight tied around their neck.\wj*
\v 43 \wj If your hand causes you to disobey God, cut it off—it’s better to enter \+add eternal\+add* life being maimed, than to have two hands but to be thrown into the fire that never goes out.\wj*
\v 44 ◘
\v 45 \wj And if your foot causes you to disobey God, cut it off—it’s better to enter \+add eternal\+add* life being maimed, than to have two feet but to be thrown into hell.\wj*
\v 46 ◘
\v 47 \wj And if your eye causes you to disobey God, gouge it out—it’s better to enter God’s kingdom one-eyed, than to have two eyes and be thrown into hell\wj*
\v 48 \wj where the worms never die and the fire never goes out.\wj*
\p
\v 49 “\wj Everyone will be salted with fire.\wj*
\p
\v 50 “\wj \+add Now\+add* salt is good, but if it were to go bad, what would you season your food with \+add then\+add*? So keep yourselves like salt, and strive to get on well with each other.\wj*”
\c 10
\s1 It’s God that joins husband and wife
\r (Mat. 19:1-12; Luke 16:18)
\p
\v 1 Then Jesus left Capernaum and went down to the Judea area and across the Jordan \add river\add*. Again the crowds followed him, and he taught them just like he usually did.
\p
\v 2 Some from the Pharisee party approached Jesus \add to check him out and\add* asked, “Does the law allow a husband to dismiss his wife?”
\p
\v 3 “\wj \+add Well\+add*, what \+add law\+add* did Moses give to you all?\wj*” he asked.
\p
\v 4 They answered, “Moses \add said that a husband is allowed\add* to write a statement of divorce and dismiss \add his wife with it\add*.”
\p
\v 5 “\wj \+add Yes,\+add* he wrote that law,\wj*” Jesus responded, “\wj because he saw your stubborn desires,\wj*
\v 6 \wj but God made \+add both\+add* men and women \+add right\+add* at the beginning.\wj*
\v 7 \wj As a result, a man leaves his parents and joins together with his wife\wj*
\v 8 \wj and the couple become a single unit. So there’s no longer two \+add of them\+add*, but \+add only\+add* one.\wj*
\v 9 \wj So if it’s God who joins them together \+add like that\+add*, no person \+add has the authority\+add* to separate them.\wj*”
\p
\v 10 \add Back\add* in the house again, his apprentices asked about that,
\v 11 and Jesus told them, “\wj Anyone who divorces his wife and marries another person, commits adultery towards his wife.\wj*”
\v 12 \wj And \+add likewise,\+add* if the wife divorces her husband \+add and\+add* marries another person, she’s committing adultery \+add towards her husband\+add*.\wj*”
\s1 Jesus encourages childlike faith
\r (Mat. 19:13-15; Luke 18:15-17)
\p
\v 13 The people were bringing small children for Jesus to touch, but his apprentices scolded them.
\v 14 However, Jesus noticed it and was upset, “\wj \+add Just\+add* let those kids come. Don’t prevent them, because God’s kingdom consists of people like them.\wj*
\v 15 \wj Actually, you can’t \+add even\+add* enter God’s kingdom unless you accept it just like a small child does.\wj*”
\v 16 So he continued hugging the children and commanding a good life for them as he placed his hands on each one.
\s1 Leaving everything behind to be saved
\r (Mat. 19:16-30; Luke 18:18-30)
\p
\v 17 One time when Jesus was starting out on a trip, a man caught up to him and kneeling down \add in front\add* of him asked, “Good teacher, what do I have to do to inherit eternal life?”
\p
\v 18 “\wj Why do you call me \+em good\+em*?\wj*” he asked. “\wj No person is good—only God \+add is\+add*.\wj*
\v 19 \wj You know God’s instructions: don’t murder, don’t commit adultery, don’t steal, don’t lie in court, don’t defraud others, and honour your parents.\wj*”
\p
\v 20 “Teacher, I’ve kept all of those since I was young,” the man responded.
\p
\v 21 Jesus looked up at him with genuine love and said, “\wj There’s one thing still to be done: sell everything you have and give the money to the poor—then you’ll have wealth in heaven. After that, come and follow me.\wj*”
\v 22 But the man grew very sad when he heard that, and he went away upset because he owned many properties.
\p
\v 23 Jesus looked around and told his followers, “\wj It’s so difficult for wealthy people to enter God’s kingdom.\wj*”
\p
\v 24 (His followers were a bit stunned at his teaching.) Jesus continued, “\wj Yes, it’s certainly difficult to enter into God’s kingdom.\wj*
\v 25 \wj It’s easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for a rich person to enter God’s kingdom.\wj*”
\p
\v 26 Now they were totally stunned, asking, “How can anyone possibly be saved then?”
\p
\v 27 Jesus looked intently at them, “\wj \+add Yes,\+add* humanly it’s \+add quite\+add* impossible. But with God it’s different—God can do anything!\wj*”
\p
\v 28 Peter said, “Well, we left everything \add and came\add* and followed you.”
\p
\v 29 “\wj Yes, I can assure you all,\wj*” Jesus answered, “\wj that anyone who left their home or farm, and left their siblings or parents or children because of me and my teaching,\wj*
\v 30 \wj they’ll be rewarded one hundred times over with homes and farms and siblings and parents and children along with times of persecution, but gaining eternal life in the next era.\wj*
\v 31 \wj But many at the top now will be at the bottom then, and many at the bottom now will be blessed then.\wj*”
\s1 Jesus’s third prediction about his execution
\r (Mat. 20:17-19; Luke 18:31-34)
\p
\v 32 They were \add heading south\add* on their way to Jerusalem with Jesus in front, and his followers were a bit surprised and a bit scared. Jesus took the twelve aside, and started telling them what was going to happen to him,
\v 33 “\wj We’re on our way down to Jerusalem and the son of humanity will be turned over to the chief priests and the religious teachers, and they’ll sentence him to be executed and hand him over to the non-Jews.\wj*
\v 34 \wj Then they’ll ridicule him and spit on him before they beat him and execute him, but he’ll come back to life again in three days \+add time\+add*.\wj*”
\s1 How to become respected in God’s kingdom
\r (Mat. 20:20-28)
\p
\v 35 Then Zebedee’s two sons, James and John, approached Jesus and asked, “Teacher, we’d like to ask a favour from you—please don’t disappoint us.”
\p
\v 36 “\wj What do you want from me?\wj*” Jesus asked.
\p
\v 37 They answered, “Allow us \add two\add* to sit beside you in heaven—one of us on each side of you.”
\p
\v 38 “\wj You don’t \+add even\+add* understand what you’re asking for,\wj*” Jesus replied. “\wj Do you think you could endure the suffering that I have to go through? Or to be enveloped in the darkness that I have to go down into?\wj*”
\p
\v 39 “Sure we can,” they replied.
\p “\wj And indeed you all will suffer like me and be enveloped in darkness like I will be,\wj*” responded Jesus,
\v 40 “\wj but as for deciding who will sit next to me, that’s not my decision to make because it’s already been decided.\wj*”
\p
\v 41 When the ten \add other apprentices\add* heard \add what those two had asked for\add*, they were upset at Jacob and John.
\v 42 But Jesus called them \add over\add*, “\wj \+add Listen,\+add* in most countries their rulers boss the people around and their leaders exert their authority over them.\wj*
\v 43 \wj But it doesn’t work like that with you—anyone of you who wants to be honoured must serve the others,\wj*
\v 44 \wj and anyone who wants to become the most respected has to \+add work\+add* like a slave does.\wj*
\v 45 \wj Even the son of humanity didn’t come to be served, but to serve others and to give his life as a ransom to set many people free.\wj*”
\s1 Blind Bartimaeus gets his miracle
\r (Mat. 20:29-34; Luke 18:35-43)
\p
\v 46 They arrived at Jericho, and then \add when\add* Jesus and his apprentices were leaving \add again\add* with a large crowd following, \add they came across\add* blind Bartimaeus Timaeus’ son) sitting beside the path \add begging\add*.
\v 47 When he heard that it was Jesus from Nazareth \add going past\add*, he yelled out, “Jesus, \add promised\add* descendant of David, help me please.”
\p
\v 48 When several people told him to stop, he just yelled out even louder, “\add Jesus, promised\add* descendant of David, please help me.”
\p
\v 49 Then Jesus stopped and got the people to call the blind man. So they called him, “\add Hey,\add* be confident! Get up \add and come because\add* he’s called for you.”
\p
\v 50 So Bartimaeus threw off his jacket, jumped up, \add and\add* was led to Jesus.
\p
\v 51 “\wj \+add Now,\+add* what do you want me to do for you?\wj*” Jesus asked.
\p “My teacher, I’d like to be able to see,” Bartimaeus replied.
\p
\v 52 “\wj \+add Okay,\+add* off you go \+add then\+add*,\wj*” Jesus responded. “\wj Your faith has saved you.\wj*”
\p Immediately Bartimaeus was able to see, and followed along the path after Jesus.
\c 11
\s1 Jesus is treated like a king entering Jerusalem
\r (Yhn 12:12-19; Mat. 21:1-11; Luke 19:28-40)
\p
\v 1 As they got close to Jerusalem and were passing Bethphage and Bethany on the Mt. of Olives, Jesus sent two of his apprentices \add ahead\add*.
\v 2 telling them, “\wj Go on to the \+add next\+add* village and as you enter it, you’ll see a young donkey that hasn’t ever been ridden yet. Untie it and bring it \+add back here\+add*.\wj*
\v 3 \wj If anyone asks what you’re doing, tell them that the master needs it and he’ll return it later.\wj*”
\p
\v 4 So they went ahead and found a donkey tied up on the path \add just\add* outside the door of a house, and \add so they went to\add* untie it.
\v 5 But some of the people standing around there said, “\add Hoy!\add* What \add do you think\add* you’re doing untying that donkey?”
\p
\v 6 So they responded \add just\add* as Jesus had told them, and the people allowed them \add to proceed\add*.
\v 7 They brought the young donkey to Jesus, placing their coats over it before Jesus mounted it.
\v 8 \add As Jesus rode it into Jerusalem,\add* many people laid their coats on the road, while others lay down leafy branches cut \add from trees\add* in \add nearby\add* fields.
\v 9 Some went ahead and others came along behind, shouting, “This is the saviour who comes with the authority and blessing from Yahweh—
\v 10 the \add promised\add* ancestor of \add King\add* David who is blessed as he ushers in his kingdom—the blessed saviour from heaven.”
\p
\v 11 And \add so\add* Jesus arrived in Jerusalem and went into the temple and looked all around, \add but\add* because it was now evening, he went back out to Bethany \add village\add* with his twelve apprentices.
\s1 Jesus curses a fig tree
\r (Mat. 21:18-19)
\p
\v 12 The next day after they’d left Bethany \add village\add*, Jesus was hungry.
\v 13 From a distance he noticed a fig tree that was in leaf \add so\add* he went over to see if there was any fruit on it, but when he reached it there was only leaves because it wasn’t the \add right\add* season for figs.
\v 14 So he spoke to the tree, “\wj No one in this age will \+add ever\+add* eat fruit from you \+add again\+add*.\wj*” (His followers heard \add him say that\add*.)
\s1 Jesus upsets the traders inside the temple
\r (Yhn 2:13-22; Mat. 21:12-17; Luke 19:45-48)
\p
\v 15 Then they came \add back\add* into Jerusalem and on into the temple where Jesus started driving out the sellers and the buyers \add that were trading\add* in there. Then he tipped over the money-changers’ tables and the chairs of the ones selling doves,
\v 16 and stopped others from carrying goods through the temple.
\v 17 He taught them, “\wj Isn’t it written \+add in the scriptures\+add*,\wj*
\q1 \wj ‘My house will be known as a prayer house for all the nations \+add to use\+add*’?\wj*
\m \wj But all of you have turned it into a sanctuary for swindlers.\wj*”
\p
\v 18 When the chief priests and the religious teachers heard that, they \add started\add* looking for a way that they could destroy him, because they were afraid of him and the way that the whole crowd marvelled at his teaching.
\p
\v 19 Every evening, Jesus and his apprentices would go out of the city \add to stay overnight\add*.
\s1 Jesus gives a lesson on moving mountains
\r (Mat. 21:20-22)
\p
\v 20 As they entered the city again in the morning, Jesus’ apprentices noticed that the fig tree was totally dead.
\v 21 Peter remembered \add what he had said to the tree\add*, \add and\add* commented, “Teacher, that fig tree that you cursed has withered up.”
\p
\v 22 “\wj Use your faith in God,\wj* Jesus responded.
\v 23 \wj I can assure you all that if anyone tells a mountain to go away into the sea, and doesn’t doubt in their mind but believes that what they’re saying will happen, \+add then\+add* it will.\wj*
\v 24 \wj So I tell you all that anything you ask \+add God\+add* for in your prayers, if you believe that you have received it, then you will.\wj*
\v 25 \wj \+add All of you,\+add* whenever you’re standing praying, if you have something against someone, be \+add sure\+add* to forgive them so that your father in heaven can also forgive your disobedience.\wj*”
\s1 The leaders question Jesus’s authority
\r (Mat. 21:23-27; Luke 20:1-8)
\p
\v 26 ◘
\v 27 They continued on into Jerusalem and on to the temple where they were met by the chief priests and the religious teachers and the elders.
\v 28 They asked Jesus, “Did you have any authority to do what you did? If so, who gave it to you?”
\p
\v 29 “\wj \+add Okay,\+add* I’ll also ask you something,\wj*” Jesus replied, \wj and \+add when you all\+add* answer me, I’ll tell you about my authority to do that:\wj*
\v 30 \wj When John \+add preached and\+add* immersed people, did the authority for that come from heaven or from people here?\wj*”
\p
\v 31 They \add discussed\add* that among themselves, reasoning, “If we say it was from heaven, he’ll ask us why we didn’t believe him \add then\add*.
\v 32 But we \add can’t\add* say that it was just from people.” (They said that because they were scared of the crowds, knowing that they believed that John really was a prophet.)
\v 33 So they answered, “We don’t know.”
\p “\wj \+add Well then,\+add*\wj*” Jesus responded, “\wj I won’t tell you about my authority to do those things either.\wj*”
\c 12
\s1 The parable about the tenants killing the owner’s son
\r (Mat. 21:33-46; Luke 20:9-19)
\p
\v 1 Then he started telling them \add some\add* parables: “\wj A man planted a vineyard and put a fence around it, dug out a wine-pressing pit, and built a \+add security\+add* tower. Then he rented it out and \+add went off\+add* travelling.\wj*
\v 2 \wj At harvest time, he sent a slave \+add to go\+add* to the tenant farmers and get his percentage of the harvest.\wj*
\v 3 \wj But the tenants grabbed the slave and beat him up, then sent him back empty-handed.\wj*
\v 4 \wj The owner sent a second slave, but they insulted that one and struck him on the head.\wj*
\v 5 \wj So the owner sent a third slave and they murdered him. He sent many other slaves—some they \+add just\+add* beat up and others they murdered.\wj*
\v 6 \wj \+add But\+add* the owner still had his one, dearly loved son, \+add so\+add* finally he sent him to go, thinking that they would now be convinced because it was his son.\wj*
\v 7 \wj However, those tenant farmers said to each other, ‘Ah ha. This is the one who will inherit \+add this land\+add*. If we kill \+em him\+em*, we’ll be the ones to control the estate.’\wj*
\v 8 \wj So they took him and killed him, and threw the body outside the vineyard \+add fence\+add*.\wj*
\p
\v 9 “\wj Now, what \+add do you think\+add* the vineyard owner will do? \+add Yes,\+add* he’ll come and eliminate those \+add evil\+add* tenants, and give the contract to others.\wj*
\v 10 \wj Haven’t you all read this in the scriptures:\wj*
\q1 \wj ‘the stone that the builders rejected\wj*
\q1 \wj became the \+add important\+add* cornerstone\wj*
\q1
\v 11 \wj that was given by Yahweh,\wj*
\q1 \wj and we think it’s incredible.’?\wj*”
\p
\v 12 Then those leaders tried to arrest him because they knew that it was them that the parable was addressed at, but \add they couldn’t do it because Jesus\add* \add had the favour\add* of the crowd, so they \add just\add* left \add the area\add*.
\s1 Jesus answers about paying taxes
\r (Mat. 22:15-22; Luke 20:20-26)
\p
\v 13 Then the religious leaders sent some of the Pharisee party members and some of the \add political\add* supporters of \add King\add* Herod to try to catch Jesus out.
\v 14 So they flattered him, “Teacher, we know that you are honest even if it upsets others, and that you teach the truth about God without caring about people’s opinions. Is it right \add for us Jews\add* to pay the poll tax to the Roman emperor or not? How should we handle this?”
\p
\v 15 However, knowing their hypocrisy, Jesus responded to them, “\wj Why are all of you \+add trying to\+add* test me? Bring me a coin so I can see it.\wj*”
\p
\v 16 When they brought \add him\add* a coin, he asked, “\wj Whose image and inscription are on this \+add coin\+add*?\wj*”
\p “The emperor’s,” they answered.
\p
\v 17 “\wj \+add Well then,\+add*\wj*” said Jesus, “\wj give the emperor whatever is due him, but give God what is owed to God.\wj*”
\p After that, they had to admit that that was an outstanding answer.
\s1 Jesus explains that those who’ve died are alive
\r (Mat. 22:23-33; Luke 20:27-40)
\p
\v 18 Another time, Sadducee group members (who say that there’s no resurrection), came to Jesus and asked,
\v 19 “Teacher, Moses wrote that if a man’s \add married\add* brother should die before his wife without having any children, then his brother should marry her to produce descendants in his brother’s name.
\v 20 \add Now what if\add* there were seven brothers and the first one got married and died childless.
\v 21 So then the second brother took her in, but \add again\add* died childless, and then too the third.
\v 22 \add Eventually all\add* seven brothers died childless, and finally the woman also died.
\v 23 \add Now,\add* at the resurrection \add of the dead\add*, which brother will she be the wife of, because \add at some point,\add* she was married to \add each of\add* the seven of them?”
\p
\v 24 “\wj Actually you’re all quite wrong,\wj*” Jesus answered, “\wj because you ignore the scriptures and \+add also\+add* because you haven’t experienced God’s power.\wj*
\v 25 \wj When the dead do come back to life, they won’t be getting engaged or married, but will be like the messengers in the heavens.\wj*
\v 26 \wj But as for the state of those who’ve died, didn’t you all read in the scriptures about Moses when God spoke to him \+add in front of\+add* the \+add burning\+add* thorn bush and said, ‘I \+em am\+em* the god of Abraham and the god of Isaac and the god of Jacob’?\wj*
\v 27 \wj \+add So\+add* God is not the god of the dead, but of the living. You Sadducee group members are very wrong!\wj*”
\s1 Jesus answers about genuine obedience to God
\r (Mat. 22:34-40; Luke 10:25-28)
\p
\v 28 Then one of the religious teachers who had heard their question and Jesus’ excellent answer, came closer \add and\add* asked, “Which of God’s commands is the most important?”
\p
\v 29 “\wj \+add Well\+add*,\wj*” Jesus answered, “\wj the most important is \+add this\+add*: ‘Listen, Israel. Our God Yahweh is our master.\wj*
\v 30 \wj You must love Yahweh God with all your heart and all your soul, and with all your mind and all your strength.’\wj*
\v 31 \wj \+add And\+add* the second is: ‘Love your neighbour like \+add you look after\+add* yourself.’ \+add There’s\+add* no other commands more important than these.\wj*”
\p
\v 32 The teacher replied to Jesus, “Yes, teacher, you’re quite right saying there’s \add only\add* one God and no other,
\v 33 and saying that \add we should\add* love him with all our heart and all our intelligence and all our strength, and to be loving our neighbours like we look after ourselves, \add and all this\add* is more important than all the required temple offerings and sacrifices.”
\p
\v 34 When Jesus saw that he’d given such a thoughtful answer, he said, “\wj You’re very close to God’s kingdom!\wj*”
\p After that, no one else dared to ask Jesus \add any more questions\add*.
\s1 Jesus asks about the messiah being David’s descendant
\r (Mat. 22:41-46; Luke 20:41-44)
\p
\v 35 Later when Jesus was teaching in the temple, he asked \add the people\add*, “\wj How is it that the teachers \+add here\+add* say that the messiah is a descendant of \+add King\+add* David?\wj*
\v 36 \wj \+add Because\+add* David himself said under the inspiration of the holy spirit,\wj*
\q1 \wj ‘Yahweh told my master,\wj*
\q2 \wj “Sit there in the position of honour\wj*
\q2 \wj until I can conquer your enemies.” ’\wj*
\m
\v 37 \wj \+add If\+add* David calls the messiah his master, how can the messiah possibly be David’s descendant?\wj*”
\p The large crowd was very interested to hear everything that Jesus \add had to say\add*.
\s1 Jesus warns about well-dressed teachers
\r (Mat. 23:1-36; Luke 20:45-47)
\p
\v 38 As Jesus continued teaching, he said, “\wj Watch out for those religious teachers who like to wear \+add fancy\+add* robes and be greeted in the square,\wj*
\v 39 \wj and \+add have the\+add* best seats at meetings and the best places at dinners.\wj*
\v 40 \wj Those people trick widows out of their properties, but offer long prayers to cover it up. They will be getting a stricter judgement \+add than others\+add*.\wj*”
\s1 A widow’s sacrificial donation
\r (Luke 21:1-4)
\p
\v 41 As he sat down in front of the \add temple\add* treasury, Jesus watched the crowd as they threw coins into the dish, and some rich people were donating quite valuable silver coins.
\v 42 Then an impoverished widow came \add and\add* threw \add in\add* two small copper coins.
\v 43 Jesus called his followers and told them, “\wj Honestly, that hard-up widow gave more than all the others.\wj*
\v 44 \wj You see, most people donate from their excess wealth, but this woman \+add even\+add* gave \+add some of\+add* the small amount that she needed to live on.\wj*”
\c 13
\s1 Jesus predicts the temple’s total destruction
\r (Mat. 24:1-2; Luke 21:5-6)
\p
\v 1 As they were leaving the temple \add area\add*, one of Jesus’ followers noted, “Teacher, look at those \add large, chiselled\add* stones and those \add beautiful\add* buildings \add that they’ve been made into\add*.”
\p
\v 2 “\wj Yes, incredible buildings,\wj*” Jesus responded, “\wj \+add but\+add* the time will come when they’re torn down—not a single \+add piece of\+add* stone will be left sitting in its place.\wj*”
\s1 Jesus talks about coming hardships
\r (Mat. 24:3-14; Luke 21:7-19)
\p
\v 3 \add Later when they were over\add* on the Mt. of Olives overlooking the temple, Peter and James and John and Andrew were \add with\add* Jesus \add and\add* asked
\v 4 when all that \add destruction\add* would happen, and what indications there would be that it would be about to happen.
\p
\v 5 But Jesus told them, “\wj Be careful so you all aren’t misled by others.\wj*
\v 6 \wj Plenty of people will say that they’re coming because they were sent by me, or that they \+em are\+em* me, and they’ll mislead many others.\wj*
\v 7 \wj Also, whenever you all hear about wars or about battles about to begin, don’t be worried—those things will certainly happen, but there’s more to follow.\wj*
\v 8 \wj Countries will take up arms against \+add other\+add* countries, and kingdoms against \+add other\+add* kingdoms. There’ll be earthquakes in \+add many\+add* places and there’ll be famines, \+add but\+add* those \+add will be just like\+add* the beginning of birth pains.\wj*
\p
\v 9 “\wj So be careful. You’ll be turned over to \+add local\+add* councils \+add for questioning\+add*, will be whipped in the Jewish meeting halls, and will be stood in front of governors and kings because of following me. However, you’ll be able to present your life stories to them\wj*
\v 10 \wj and it’s right that the good message first\wj*\f + \fr 13:10 \ft TD: It’s not clear here what was meant by ‘first’—‘first’ before what?\f* \wj be declared to every people group.\wj*
\v 11 \wj But whenever they take you all along to hand you over, don’t worry in advance \+add about\+add* what you’re going to say \+add to defend yourselves\+add*, but wait until the hearing proceeds and speak what you are given then, because then it won’t be you speaking, but God’s spirit.\wj*
\v 12 \wj \+add In those times,\+add* a brother will report on his brother to have him executed, and a father report on his child, and children will be encouraged to report on their \+add own\+add* parents and then they’ll be executed \+add as a result\+add*.\wj*
\v 13 \wj Then every one will hate you all because \+add they hate\+add* my name, but anyone who endures to the end will be saved.\wj*
\s1 Jesus warns about coming horror and deceptions
\r (Mat. 24:15-28; Luke 21:20-24)
\p
\v 14 “\wj But whenever you all see the most horrible thing that drives people \+add from God\+add*, standing where it shouldn’t be (\+add and you\+add* readers should understand that), then anyone in Judea should flee into the hills,\wj*
\v 15 \wj and anyone upstairs \+add looking outside\+add* shouldn’t come down into \+add the bedrooms\+add* to get anything,\wj*
\v 16 \wj and anyone working outside shouldn’t go back inside to get a change of clothes.\wj*
\v 17 \wj It will be extremely difficult for pregnant women and for mothers who are breast-feeding \+add their children\+add* in those days.\wj*
\v 18 \wj Just pray that it won’t happen in the winter,\wj*
\v 19 \wj because those days will be terrible like nothing that’s ever happened before, all the way back to when God created \+add the world, right\+add* until the present time, and won’t ever happen \+add again\+add*.\wj*
\v 20 \wj If Yahweh hadn’t shortened that time, no humans would even survive, but he shortened that time so that the chosen ones \+add could be saved\+add*.\wj*
\p
\v 21 \wj So don’t believe it if you all \+add hear\+add* someone say, ‘Look, the messiah \+add has arrived\+add* here.’ or ‘There’s the one \+add that God\+add* has sent.’\wj*
\v 22 \wj \+add Plenty of\+add* fake messiahs and deceiving prophets will \+add certainly\+add* come and \+add even\+add* do things that humans can’t normally do, so much so that \+add even\+add* the chosen ones would defect if that were possible.\wj*
\v 23 \wj So watch out, \+add but\+add* I’ve warned you in advance \+add about\+add* all of that.\wj*
\s1 The universe collapses as the son arrives
\r (Mat. 24:29-31; Luke 21:25-28)
\p
\v 24 “\wj After those very difficult times, the sun will go dark and the moon won’t shine \+add at night\+add*.\wj*
\v 25 \wj The stars will fall \+add down\+add* out of the sky and the powers in the heavens will be shaken \+add up\+add*.\wj*
\v 26 \wj Then the people will see the son of humanity in the clouds coming with incredible power and brightness.\wj*
\v 27 \wj He’ll send the messengers out to collect all the chosen people from the four corners of the world—from everywhere between the earth and the sky.\wj*
\s1 Watch out for the change in season
\r (Mat. 24:32-35; Luke 21:29-33)
\p
\v 28 “\wj So learn a lesson from fig trees—whenever the branches green up and start sprouting leaves, then everyone knows that summer is close.\wj*
\v 29 \wj So too whenever you see all those things happening, you know that the end of this current world is close.\wj*
\v 30 \wj I can assure you all that the generation \+add of people who see those things\+add* won’t die off before those \+add latter\+add* things happen.\wj*
\v 31 \wj The sky and the planet might collapse, but my teachings will never become irrelevant.\wj*
\s1 Stay alert for the coming of the master
\r (Mat. 24:36-44)
\p
\v 32 “\wj But as for the date or time \+add when all that will happen\+add*, only the father knows that—even the son and the messengers in heaven don’t know.\wj*
\v 33 \wj \+add So\+add* watch out, observe \+add current events\+add*, and be praying, because you won’t know exactly when all this will happen.\wj*
\v 34 \wj It can all be compared to a man who \+add was about\+add* to leave his home and go on a trip. \+add Before he leaves,\+add* he gives \+add certain\+add* jobs and responsibilities to his \+add various\+add* slaves, and instructs his security guard to stay alert.\wj*
\v 35 \wj So you all watch \+add and stay alert\+add*, because you don’t know when the master of the house might return—\+add perhaps one\+add* evening or at midnight, or at dawn or maybe \+add mid-\+add*morning.\wj*
\v 36 \wj You \+add certainly\+add* don’t want to be caught napping \+add when\+add* he comes,\wj*
\v 37 \wj and what I’m telling all of you \+add now\+add*, I tell everyone: \+add stay alert and\+add* watch.\wj*”
\c 14
\s1 The leaders plan to eliminate Jesus
\r (Yhn 11:45-53; Mat. 26:1-5; Luke 22:1-2)
\p
\v 1 Now it was two days before the Passover Feast and the \it Flat Bread \+add Celebration\+add*\it*, and the chief priests and the religious teachers were trying to figure out how they could have Jesus set up so they could arrest him and have him executed,
\v 2 but they knew that the Feast wouldn’t be a good time because the people would protest \add in Jesus’s favour\add*.
\s1 The woman anoints Jesus for his burial
\r (Yhn 12:1-8; Mat. 26:6-13)
\p
\v 3 Meanwhile, Jesus was in Bethany at the home of Simon who \add had previously\add* had leprosy. Jesus was sitting \add there when\add* a woman came \add in\add* with a hand-crafted container of very expensive fragrant lotion. Breaking \add open\add* the \add sealed\add* flask, she poured the fragrant oil over Jesus’s head.
\v 4 \add Immediately\add* some \add in the room started\add* quietly grumbling about the extravagant waste,
\v 5 then they scolded the woman because \add a flask of genuine\add* lotion like that could \add easily\add* fetch almost a year’s wages if it was sold.
\p
\v 6 But Jesus spoke up, “\wj What do you think you’re all doing abusing her—leave her \+add alone\+add*! She’s done something very good for me.\wj*
\v 7 \wj You’ll always have poor people around, and you can help them whenever you want to, but I won’t be around forever.\wj*
\v 8 \wj She has performed the \+add traditional\+add* anointing of my body in advance of my burial,\wj*
\v 9 \wj and I can assure you all, that wherever the good message is declared around the world, what she has done \+add today\+add* will be part of the story and she’ll be remembered for it.\wj*”
\s1 Judea offers to hand Jesus over
\r (Mat. 26:14-16; Luke 22:3-6)
\p
\v 10 Then Judas Iscariot, one of Jesus’ twelve apprentices, visited the chief priests to offer to hand Jesus over to them.
\v 11 When they heard that, they were very pleased and promised to pay him well, and Judas asked them about the most convenient way for them to get hold of Jesus.
\s1 Jesus’s apprentices prepare the Passover meal
\r (Yhn 13:21-30; Mat. 26:17-25; Luke 22:7-14, 21-23)
\p
\v 12 On the first day of the \it Flat Bread \+add Celebration\+add*\it* when \add the lambs\add* would be slaughtered ready for the special Passover meal, Jesus’ apprentices asked him, “Where do you want us to get everything ready for our Passover meal?”
\p
\v 13 So Jesus sent off two of them with these instructions, “\wj Go into town and a man carrying a pitcher of water will meet you there. \+add Just\+add* follow him\wj*
\v 14 \wj until he goes into \+add a house\+add*. Then \+add find\+add* the owner of the house and tell him that the teacher wants to know \+add the location of\+add* the guestroom where he and his apprentices can celebrate their Passover meal.\wj*
\v 15 \wj He’ll show you a large, furnished room upstairs—that’s where you can get everything ready.\wj*”
\p
\v 16 So the two of them went off into the town, and everything happened there \add exactly\add* as Jesus had told them, and so they prepared the special meal there.
\s1 Jesus shocks his apprentices at the Passover meal
\r (Yhn 13:21-30; Mat. 26:20-30; Luke 22:14-23; 1 Cor. 11:23-25)
\p
\v 17 As the evening approached, Jesus went in \add for dinner\add* with his twelve apprentices.
\v 18 As they were sitting around eating, he told them, “\wj I’ll tell you frankly that one of you eating with me \+add here\+add* will hand me over \+add to be arrested\+add*.\wj*”
\v 19 That upset them, and several of them individually asked, “Surely it couldn’t be me?”
\p
\v 20 Then he continued, “\wj It’s one of you twelve—the one who dips his bread in the sauce at the same time as me.\wj*
\v 21 \wj \+add Indeed\+add* it’s written \+add in the scriptures\+add* that the son of humanity will be arrested, but on the other hand, what a tragedy it’ll be for the one who hands him over—\+add in fact\+add* it would be better for that man if he was never born.\wj*”
\p
\v 22 As they \add carried on\add* eating, \add he\add* took \add some\add* bread, blessed \add God\add*, broke it \add apart\add* and gave \add a piece\add* to them, saying, “\wj Take this bread—it’s my body.\wj*”
\p
\v 23 Then he picked up a cup, gave thanks \add to God\add*, \add and\add* passed it around so they could each drink from it,
\v 24 saying, “\wj That is the blood of my contract that will be poured out for many people.\wj*
\v 25 \wj I can assure you all that I won’t drink anything made from grapes until I drink new juice in God’s kingdom.\wj*”
\p
\v 26 Then after they’d sung \add some\add* songs, they left the town and went \add across\add* to the Mt. of Olives.
\s1 Jesus predicts Peters denials
\r (Yhn 13:36-38; Mat. 26:31-35; Luke 22:31-34)
\p
\v 27 Then Jesus \add continued\add* his teaching, “\wj All of you will stumble because it’s written \+add in the scriptures\+add*: ‘I’ll strike \+add down\+add* the shepherd and the sheep will get scattered.’\wj*
\v 28 \wj But after I come back to life, I’ll go on ahead of you all \+add back\+add* to Galilee.\wj*”
\p
\v 29 But Peter contradicted him, “Even if everyone \add else\add* stumbles, I won’t!”
\p
\v 30 “\wj I can assure you,\wj*” Jesus responded, “\wj that you’ll renounce me three times this \+add very\+add* night, before the rooster crows \+add early tomorrow morning\+add*.\wj*”
\p
\v 31 But Peter argued forcibly, “Even if I had to die with you, I would never disown you!”
\p And all \add the others\add* said similar things.
\s1 Jesus, in distress, prays at Gethsemane
\r (Mat. 26:36-46; Luke 22:39-46)
\p
\v 32 They \add walked along until\add* they came to a piece of land called Gethsemane where Jesus told his apprentices to sit down while he prayed.
\v 33 He took Peter and James and John \add further in\add* with him where he stared off in a daze but was \add obviously very\add* distressed.
\v 34 He told \add the three of\add* them, “\wj My soul is deathly sad! \+add You guys just\+add* stay here and keep alert.\wj*”
\p
\v 35 He went ahead a little \add way\add*, where he knelt on the ground \add with his head down\add* and prayed—\add asking\add* if it was possible to avoid what was ahead of him.
\v 36 But he said, “\wj Father, I know that you can do anything. Let me avoid this suffering, but \+add yes I do know, it doesn’t matter\+add* what I want but rather what you’ve planned.\wj*”
\p
\v 37 Then he went back and found the others sleeping. “\wj Simon, are you asleep?\wj*” he asked Peter. “\wj Couldn’t you stay awake \+add even for\+add* a single hour?\wj*
\v 38 \wj You all \+add need to\+add* stay alert and be careful not to give into temptation. I know your spirits are enthusiastic on one hand, but it’s still easy to give in to the moment.\wj*”
\p
\v 39 Then he went further in \add again and\add* prayed as before,
\v 40 but when he returned again, he found them all asleep because they were tired, but they couldn’t really find any excuse to give.
\p
\v 41 The third time that Jesus returned, he said to them, “\wj \+add Ah,\+add* you’re all sleeping again and having a \+add good\+add* rest. It’s well-earned \+add but\+add* look, the time has come for the son of humanity to be handed over to evil men.\wj*
\v 42 \wj Come on—get up—we \+add need\+add* to go! Look, the one who’ll hand me over is almost here.\wj*”
\s1 Jesus is arrested in the night
\r (Yhn 18:3-12; Mat. 26:47-56; Luke 22:47-53)
\p
\v 43 Even while Jesus was still speaking, Judas one of his twelve apprentices) reached him, followed by a crowd that included some of the chief priests, the religious teachers, and the \add Jewish\add* elders—\add many armed\add* with swords and clubs.
\v 44 Judas had prearranged a signal with them that the one he \add greeted with a\add* kiss, they should grab that person and lead him away under guard.
\p
\v 45 So he walked right up to Jesus \add and\add* said, “My honoured teacher,” and \add greeted\add* him with a kiss.
\v 46 Then the others with him grabbed Jesus and secured him.
\v 47 But one of the ones \add with Jesus\add* that was beside him, drew his sword and struck one of the chief priest’s slaves—cutting off the man’s ear.
\v 48 Jesus spoke to the crowd, “\wj Did you all bring swords and clubs \+add to try\+add* to capture me \+add like you’d chase down\+add* a thief?\wj*
\v 49 \wj I was teaching \+add there\+add* in the temple every day and you could have arrested me, but \+add it’s happened this way\+add* to ensure that \+add what’s written in\+add* scriptures will be fulfilled.\wj*”
\p
\v 50 Meanwhile, the others who’d been with Jesus had taken off.
\v 51 One young fellow was wearing a \add single\add* linen robe, and when the crowd \add tried to\add* grab him,
\v 52 it came off \add in the struggle\add* and he ran away \add stark\add* naked.
\s1 The mock trial where Jesus is sentenced to death
\r (Yhn 18:13-14,19-24; Mat. 26:57-68; Luke 22:54-55,63-71)
\p
\v 53 The guards led Jesus away to the chief priest’s \add residence\add*, and all the chief priests and the elders and the religious teachers gathered there \add also\add*.
\v 54 Peter followed along from a distance and went in as far as the courtyard of that residence, where he \add joined\add* the servants who were sitting around the fire keeping warm.
\v 55 \add Inside,\add* the chief priests and all the council were trying to establish a case against Jesus so he could be sentenced to death, but they weren’t coming up with anything.
\v 56 Several people were making up charges against him, but none of the accusers’ stories agreed with each other.
\p
\v 57 Eventually some of them stood up and offered \add this\add* false charge:
\v 58 “We heard him say that he would demolish this temple made by many labourers, and within three days he’d build another one without any helpers.”
\v 59 But even then, they couldn’t get their accusations to align with each other.
\p
\v 60 Nevertheless, the chief priest in the middle \add of the council\add* stood up and asked Jesus, “Aren’t you going to defend yourself? These men are accusing you \add of those things\add*.”
\p
\v 61 But Jesus didn’t say anything, \add so\add* the chief priest asked again, “Is it true that you’re the chosen one \add that God promised to send\add*—the son of the blessed God?”
\p
\v 62 “\wj I am.\wj*” Jesus replied, “\wj and you’ll all see the son of humanity sitting in the honoured position by the powerful \+add God\+add* and coming through the clouds in the sky.\wj*”
\p
\v 63 Then the chief priest tore his own robe \add to show his disgust\add* and shouted, “We \add certainly\add* don’t need any more witnesses \add now\add*!
\v 64 You \add yourselves\add* all heard him insult God. What sentence does that deserve?”
\p So the leaders there agreed that Jesus \add needed to be\add* sentenced to death.
\p
\v 65 Then various people started spitting on him, and blindfolding him and beating him saying, “\add Ha,\add* tell us who that was!” Even the servants \add joined in\add* to slap him.
\s1 Peter disowns Jesus
\r (Yhn 18:15-18,25-27; Mat. 26:69-75; Luke 22:56-62)
\p
\v 66 Meanwhile, Peter was down in the courtyard \add when\add* one of the chief priest’s servant girls came \add outside\add*,
\v 67 and seeing Peter warming himself, she looked more closely and said, “\add Hey,\add* you were with \add that\add* Jesus from Nazareth!”
\p
\v 68 But he denied it, “I don’t know him and I don’t know why you think I would!” Then he moved away from the fire.
\p
\v 69 But the girl that had seen him said to some of the others standing around, “That guy’s \add one\add* of them.”
\p
\v 70 But again Peter denied it.
\p A little while later, some of those standing there said the same thing to him, “You must be one of them because you’re from Galilee.”
\p
\v 71 Now Peter started swearing and cursing \add and said\add*, “I’ve never even met that fellow that you’re all talking about!”
\p
\v 72 Right then the rooster crowed for the second time and Peter remembered \add now\add* what Jesus had told him—\add how\add* he would renounce Jesus three times before the rooster crowed—and he broke down \add and\add* cried.
\c 15
\s1 Pilate tries to interrogate Jesus
\r (Yhn 18:28-38; Mat. 27:1-2,11-14; Luke 23:1-5)
\p
\v 1 First thing in the morning, the chief priests called a meeting with the elders and the religious teachers and all the council, and they ordered that Jesus be tied up and taken over to \add Governor\add* Pilate.
\v 2 \add There\add* Pilate asked him, “Are you the king of the Jews?”
\p “\wj You said it,\wj*” replied Jesus.
\p
\v 3 Then the chief priests \add started\add* hurling accusations against Jesus,
\v 4 so Pilate asked him again, “Don’t you have anything to say? Listen to all those accusations they’re making against you.”
\p
\v 5 But Pilate was astounded that Jesus didn’t answer any further.
\s1 Pilate agrees to execute an innocent man
\r (Yhn 18:39–19:16; Mat. 27:15-26; Luke 23:13-25)
\p
\v 6 Now at time of the \add Jewish\add* Passover, at their request, Pilate would \add normally\add* pardon one prisoner for them.
\v 7 There was \add another prisoner at that time\add* named Barabbas, who was an activist arrested for murder.
\v 8 By now the crowd had arrived, and asked Pilate \add to release a prisoner\add* as usual \add at Passover\add*,
\v 9 so he asked them, “Do you all want me to release the King of the Jews for you?”
\v 10 (\add That was\add* because Pilate was \add well\add* aware that the chief priests had brought Jesus because they were jealous \add of his popularity\add*.)
\v 11 But the chief priests stirred up the crowd \add to start saying\add* that it was Barabbas that they wanted released,
\v 12 so Pilate asked again, “Then what should I do \add with\add* this man that you call the King of the Jews?”
\p
\v 13 The crowd yelled back, “Execute him on a post.”
\p
\v 14 “What has he even done wrong?” Pilate demanded.
\p But they \add just\add* yelled louder, “Execute him on a post!”
\p
\v 15 Now Pilate wanted to placate the crowd, so he had Barabbas released while he had Jesus flogged and then handed over to the execution squad.
\s1 The battalion plays with their mock ‘king’
\r (Yhn 19:2-3; Mat. 27:27-31)
\p
\v 16 So the soldiers led Jesus inside the palace that was the governor’s residence, and called in the rest of the battalion.
\v 17 They \add mocked Jesus\add* by dressing him in a kingly robe, and after \add someone\add* twisted some thorns together into a \add mock\add* crown, they pressed it onto his head,
\v 18 and began saluting him, “Greetings, king of the Jews.”
\v 19 They \add took turns\add* whacking his head with a stick and spitting on him, \add while\add* others knelt down in front of him and \add pretended\add* to be bowing to him.
\v 20 \add After they’d finished having fun\add* mocking him, they took the purple robe off him, and put his own robe \add back\add* on him. Then they led him outside towards the execution area.
\s1 Jesus is publicly executed beside thieves
\r (Yhn 19:17-27; Mat. 27:32-44; Luke 23:26-43)
\p
\v 21 \add As they walked out of the city,\add* they \add came across\add* a man coming in from the country. \add He was\add* Simon from Cyrene \add Cyrene\add*—the father of Alexander and Rufus), and \add the soldiers\add* forced him to carry the execution post,
\v 22 and \add so\add* they \add all\add* arrived at Golgotha (which means ‘Place of the skull’).
\v 23 \add There\add* the soldiers \add went to\add* give \add some\add* spiced wine to Jesus, but he declined it.
\v 24 So they carried out the execution, taking Jesus’s clothes and drawing straws to see who would get what.
\v 25 By now it was around 9am, and they affixed Jesus to the post.
\v 26 The charge had been written out and \add attached at the top\add*: “The king of the Jews.”
\v 27 \add That morning,\add* two thieves were \add also\add* executed there—one on each side of Jesus.
\p
\v 28 ◘
\v 29 As people passed by \add on that path\add*, they insulted Jesus, shaking their heads and shouting, “Hoy there! You \add who reckoned\add* you could demolish the temple and rebuild it in three days,
\v 30 come down from that post \add and\add* save yourself.”
\p
\v 31 Similarly the chief priests and the religious teachers \add laughed\add* together and mocked, “He did miracles for others, but he can’t \add even\add* save himself.
\v 32 \add Now\add* if that \add so-called\add* ‘chosen one’ or ‘king of Israel’ would \add just\add* come down from there, \em then\em* we’d \add be able\add* to believe him.”
\p Even the other \add two\add* being executed with him were deriding him.
\s1 Jesus breathes his last
\r (Yhn 19:28-30; Mat. 27:45-56; Luke 23:44-49)
\p
\v 33 At noon, it got dark everywhere, until \add around\add* 3pm,
\v 34 when Jesus shouted loudly, “\wj My god, my god, why have you abandoned me?\wj*”
\p
\v 35 Some of the people standing nearby thought he was calling \add the prophet\add* Elijah.
\v 36 One of them ran, and soaked a sponge in some sour wine, then put it on a stick and held it up for Jesus to drink, saying, “Hang on. Let’s see if Elijah does come to get him down.”
\p
\v 37 But \add just then\add*, Jesus uttered a loud cry and exhaled his last breath.
\p
\v 38 (\add Back\add* in the temple, the curtain tore down the middle from the top to the bottom.)
\v 39 When Jesus died, the \add Roman\add* commander who had been standing \add back watching everything\add* said, “That man was definitely God’s son.”
\p
\v 40 Further back, there was also \add a group of\add* women observing everything. \add They\add* included Mary from Magdalene, Mary the mother of young James and Joses, and Salome,
\v 41 and others who had followed Jesus around Galilee and helped look after him, along with many others who had come down to Jerusalem with him.
\s1 Joseph boldly lays the body in a burial chamber
\r (Yhn 19:38-42; Mat. 27:57-61; Luke 23:50-56)
\p
\v 42 As evening came, it was \add now\add* the Preparation Day, i.e., the day before the Rest Day.
\v 43 Joseph who’d come from Arimathea \add city\add* and one who was waiting for the \add coming of\add* God’s kingdom), a prominent council member, steeled himself up and went in to see the governor to get permission \add to transfer\add* Jesus’ body.
\v 44 \add Governor\add* Pilate didn’t think he’d be dead yet, so he called an army commander to find out if Jesus had indeed died.
\v 45 As soon as he found out, he gave Joseph permission,
\v 46 so Joseph \add and his companions\add* went and bought some linen \add burial\add* cloth and then, removing Jesus’s body from the post, wrapped it in the cloth and placed it in a burial chamber that had been chiselled into a rockface. Then they rolled a rock across the opening.
\v 47 \add Others\add* were watching \add and noted\add* where the body had been laid, \add including\add* Mary from Magdalene and Mary who was Joses’ mother.
\c 16
\s1 The massive shock at the burial chamber
\r (Yhn 20:1-10; Mat. 28:1-8; Luke 24:1-12)
\p
\v 1 After the Rest Day was over, Mary from Magdalene, Mary the mother of James, and Salome, \add all\add* went and bought \add some traditional burial\add* spices \add that evening\add* to take \add to the burial chamber and\add* place with the body.
\v 2 Then very early on Sunday \add morning\add* they went to the chamber, arriving \add just as\add* the sun was rising.
\v 3 Then they realised that they didn’t have anyone with them to roll the rock away that was covering the entrance.
\v 4 However when they looked, they saw that the very large rock had \add already\add* been rolled away,
\v 5 so they went on into the burial chamber and were totally stunned to find a young man dressed in white, sitting \add in there\add* at the side.
\p
\v 6 He assured them, “Don’t be surprised. \add I know\add* you’re looking for Jesus from Nazareth, the one who was executed on a post. \add But\add* see \add there\add* where his body was—he’s not there, because he came back to life.
\v 7 So \add you three\add* should go and tell his apprentices, and Peter \add in particular\add*, that he’s going to go \add up\add* ahead of you all to Galilee and you’ll see him \add up there\add*, \add just\add* as he already told you all.”
\p
\v 8 So they left the chamber and took off running, \add still\add* stunned and shaking, and too scared \add to stop and\add* talk to anyone.
\rem It’s not certain whether or not the following longer ending of this document was actually penned by the same author (Markos/Mark), or whether it was appended slightly later. Expert scholars are divided on this, and hence it’s included here for your consideration:
\s1 \add The others don’t believe that Mary saw Jesus\add*
\r (Yhn 20:11-18; Mat. 28:9-10)
\p
\v 9 \add After coming back to life early on Sunday morning, Jesus\add* \add appeared first to Mary\add* \add from Magdalene. (She was the one that he had once commanded seven demons to leave.)\add*
\v 10 \add After leaving him, she went and told his followers who were mourning and weeping at his loss.\add*
\v 11 \add But when she told them that he is alive again and that she’d seen him, they simply didn’t believe her.\add*
\s1 \add Jesus appears to two of his followers\add*
\r (Luke 24:13-35)
\p
\v 12 \add Later on, Jesus\add* \add appeared in a different form to two of his followers on the path as they left Jerusalem\add* \add and headed out to the countryside,\add*
\v 13 \add but when they hurried back and told the others, no one believed them.\add*
\s1 \add Jesus appears to his apprentices and instructs them\add*
\r (Yhn 20:19-23; Mat. 28:16-20; Luke 24:36-49; Acts 1:6-8)
\p
\v 14 \add Later again when the eleven apprentices were sitting around eating, Jesus\add* \add appeared in the room and scolded them for not believing and for being so stubborn when they hadn’t believed others who had told them that he’d come back from the dead.\add*
\v 15 \add Then he instructed them, “\+wj Travel around all the world\+wj*\add* \add \+wj and tell everyone the good message.\+wj*\add*
\v 16 \add \+wj Anyone who believes and gets immersed in water will be saved, however, those who refuse to believe will be condemned to death.\+wj*\add*
\v 17 \add \+wj Those who believe will be able to do miracles: they’ll be able to use my authority to command demons to leave and to speak new languages,\+wj*\add*
\v 18 \add \+wj and they’ll be able to pick up snakes. If they drink anything poisonous, it won’t hurt them, and they will place their hands on people and make them better.\+wj*”\add*
\s1 \add Jesus is lifted up to heaven\add*
\r (Luke 24:50-53; Acts 1:9-11)
\p
\v 19 \add When Jesus\add* \add had finished speaking, he was lifted up into heaven and seated in the honoured place\add* \add next to God.\add*
\v 20 \add Then his followers went around preaching everywhere, and God worked through them and so they were able to confirm the truth of their preaching by doing miracles.\add*
